1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,500
LECTURE 1 — FUNDAMENTALS AND THE SYSTEM BOUNDARY

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,716
Here's the question that decides whether you get into the room at all.

3
00:00:09,988 --> 00:00:15,605
A Fortune five hundred procurement team opens your vendor security questionnaire, and before they

4
00:00:15,877 --> 00:00:21,494
ask anything about your architecture, before they ask about your team, before they ask

5
00:00:21,767 --> 00:00:24,976
a single technical question, they ask one thing.

6
00:00:25,249 --> 00:00:28,057
Do you have a SOC two report.

7
00:00:28,329 --> 00:00:29,934
Not are you secure.

8
00:00:30,207 --> 00:00:32,213
Do you have the report.

9
00:00:32,485 --> 00:00:38,102
That one distinction is the entire subject of this course, and once it clicks,

10
00:00:38,374 --> 00:00:39,979
everything else gets easier.

11
00:00:40,252 --> 00:00:43,060
SOC two is not a security program.

12
00:00:43,332 --> 00:00:48,949
It is a reporting standard about a security program, built by the American Institute

13
00:00:49,222 --> 00:00:50,827
of Certified Public Accountants.

14
00:00:51,099 --> 00:00:56,716
An independent CPA firm examines your controls against five Trust Service Criteria and issues

15
00:00:56,988 --> 00:00:58,192
a professional opinion.

16
00:00:58,464 --> 00:01:04,081
And here's the part that surprises almost every engineer the first time they hear

17
00:01:04,354 --> 00:01:05,254
it.

18
00:01:05,526 --> 00:01:07,532
There is no fixed checklist.

19
00:01:07,804 --> 00:01:13,421
Unlike ISO twenty seven thousand one, which hands you a fixed control set, or

20
00:01:13,694 --> 00:01:19,310
PCI DSS, which hands you fixed requirements, SOC two gives you criteria and something

21
00:01:19,583 --> 00:01:23,194
called points of focus, which are illustrative, not mandatory.

22
00:01:23,466 --> 00:01:25,071
You design the controls.

23
00:01:25,343 --> 00:01:30,960
The auditor's job is to test whether your controls satisfy the criteria, not whether

24
00:01:31,233 --> 00:01:36,448
you match some template somebody wrote in an office in Durham, North Carolina.

25
00:01:36,721 --> 00:01:42,338
This is why two completely different SOC two reports, from two completely different companies,

26
00:01:42,610 --> 00:01:46,221
can both be entirely valid, while looking nothing alike.

27
00:01:46,493 --> 00:01:52,110
And here's the second thing that changes how you think about the whole engagement.

28
00:01:52,382 --> 00:01:56,395
The report belongs to a person, not just a company.

29
00:01:56,667 --> 00:02:02,284
Somebody, usually your CTO, sometimes a named system owner, signs what's called a management

30
00:02:02,556 --> 00:02:03,456
assertion.

31
00:02:03,729 --> 00:02:05,333
It's a written claim.

32
00:02:05,606 --> 00:02:11,223
It says the controls are suitably designed, for a Type One report, or operating

33
00:02:11,495 --> 00:02:13,902
effectively, for a Type Two report.

34
00:02:14,175 --> 00:02:18,588
The auditor is opining on whether that assertion is fairly stated.

35
00:02:18,860 --> 00:02:24,076
They are not auditing your infrastructure directly, in the sense you might imagine.

36
00:02:24,348 --> 00:02:29,965
They are testing whether the claim your own leadership made about your own system

37
00:02:30,238 --> 00:02:31,138
holds up.

38
00:02:31,410 --> 00:02:37,027
Get this backwards, and you will misread every single interaction with your auditor for

39
00:02:37,299 --> 00:02:39,305
the rest of the engagement.

40
00:02:39,578 --> 00:02:45,195
Let's talk about the five criteria, precisely, because vague explanations are how teams end

41
00:02:45,467 --> 00:02:47,072
up over scoping later.

42
00:02:47,344 --> 00:02:52,961
Security is the mandatory core, the common criteria, labeled C C one through C

43
00:02:53,234 --> 00:02:57,647
C nine, and every SOC two report includes it, no exceptions.

44
00:02:57,919 --> 00:03:03,536
Then you have four optional criteria that bolt on depending on what you actually

45
00:03:03,809 --> 00:03:04,709
do.

46
00:03:04,981 --> 00:03:10,598
Availability, labeled A one, is about whether your system meets its uptime and performance

47
00:03:10,870 --> 00:03:11,770
commitments.

48
00:03:12,043 --> 00:03:17,659
Processing Integrity, labeled P I one, is about whether your processing is complete, valid,

49
00:03:17,932 --> 00:03:19,537
accurate, timely, and authorized.

50
00:03:19,809 --> 00:03:25,426
Confidentiality, labeled C one, is about whether information you've designated confidential stays that way,

51
00:03:25,698 --> 00:03:28,908
per whatever agreement you made with your customer.

52
00:03:29,180 --> 00:03:34,797
And Privacy, labeled P one through P nine, is about whether personal information is

53
00:03:35,070 --> 00:03:40,687
collected, used, retained, disclosed, and disposed of in a way that actually matches your

54
00:03:40,959 --> 00:03:42,163
stated privacy notice.

55
00:03:42,435 --> 00:03:48,052
For our reference system in this course, the Financial RAG Agent, we select all

56
00:03:48,324 --> 00:03:49,224
five.

57
00:03:49,497 --> 00:03:51,503
Not because more is impressive.

58
00:03:51,775 --> 00:03:55,787
Because each one is triggered by something specific and real.

59
00:03:56,060 --> 00:04:01,676
We have a ninety nine point nine percent uptime SLA in our enterprise contracts,

60
00:04:01,949 --> 00:04:03,554
so Availability is triggered.

61
00:04:03,826 --> 00:04:09,443
We process SEC filings and answer financial questions, where accuracy actually matters to someone's

62
00:04:09,715 --> 00:04:12,524
investment decision, so Processing Integrity is triggered.

63
00:04:12,796 --> 00:04:18,413
Users ask questions that could reveal their own investment strategy, so Confidentiality is triggered.

64
00:04:18,685 --> 00:04:23,500
We collect user emails for API key management, so Privacy is triggered.

65
00:04:23,772 --> 00:04:29,389
Every criteria you select needs a reason you can say out loud to an

66
00:04:29,661 --> 00:04:31,266
auditor in one sentence.

67
00:04:31,539 --> 00:04:36,353
If you can't produce that sentence, you probably don't need the criteria.

68
00:04:36,626 --> 00:04:42,242
Now, briefly, Type One versus Type Two, because we'll go deep on this in

69
00:04:42,515 --> 00:04:46,928
a later lecture, but you need the shape of it now.

70
00:04:47,201 --> 00:04:49,207
Type One answers one question.

71
00:04:49,479 --> 00:04:54,293
Are your controls suitably designed, as of a single point in time.

72
00:04:54,566 --> 00:04:57,374
Type Two answers a completely different question.

73
00:04:57,647 --> 00:05:03,263
Have your controls been operating effectively, over an observation period, typically six to twelve

74
00:05:03,536 --> 00:05:04,436
months.

75
00:05:04,708 --> 00:05:07,116
That's not a difference in thoroughness.

76
00:05:07,388 --> 00:05:13,005
It's a different question, full stop, and confusing the two is the single most

77
00:05:13,277 --> 00:05:16,487
expensive category of mistake in this entire field.

78
00:05:16,759 --> 00:05:22,376
Here's something almost nobody teaches at this stage, and it's exactly the kind of

79
00:05:22,649 --> 00:05:28,265
thing that separates a course written about SOC two from one written by someone

80
00:05:28,538 --> 00:05:34,155
who has actually sat across the table from a Schellman or an A-LIGN auditor.

81
00:05:34,427 --> 00:05:36,032
You depend on AWS.

82
00:05:36,304 --> 00:05:37,909
You depend on OpenAI.

83
00:05:38,182 --> 00:05:39,786
You depend on Groq.

84
00:05:40,059 --> 00:05:43,670
What happens to those dependencies in your system description.

85
00:05:43,942 --> 00:05:49,559
There are two paths here, and getting this wrong costs you real time later.

86
00:05:49,831 --> 00:05:55,448
The carve out method means you exclude the subservice organization's own controls from your

87
00:05:55,721 --> 00:06:01,337
description, and you disclose plainly that certain criteria are met using their controls, which

88
00:06:01,610 --> 00:06:07,227
your customer's auditor, or you yourself, considers separately through the subservice organization's own report,

89
00:06:07,499 --> 00:06:12,313
or through something called a bridge letter covering any gap in dates.

90
00:06:12,586 --> 00:06:18,203
The inclusive method means you fold their controls directly into your own description, and

91
00:06:18,475 --> 00:06:20,481
your auditor tests them directly.

92
00:06:20,754 --> 00:06:22,760
Nobody does this with AWS.

93
00:06:23,032 --> 00:06:23,932
Nobody should.

94
00:06:24,204 --> 00:06:29,821
For AWS, you carve out, and you back that up with AWS Artifact access

95
00:06:30,094 --> 00:06:33,303
to their own SOC two Type Two report.

96
00:06:33,576 --> 00:06:39,193
For OpenAI and for Groq, you also carve out, backed by their own attestations

97
00:06:39,465 --> 00:06:45,082
when they have one, and when they don't yet, which is genuinely common with

98
00:06:45,354 --> 00:06:50,971
fast moving AI vendors, you back it with a security questionnaire and a signed

99
00:06:51,244 --> 00:06:54,854
Data Processing Addendum, which functions as your compensating control.

100
00:06:55,127 --> 00:07:00,744
Write this decision down explicitly, in the system boundary document itself, because auditors ask

101
00:07:01,016 --> 00:07:06,633
for it in the very first prepared by client request, and having no answer

102
00:07:06,905 --> 00:07:10,115
is a rough way to open an engagement.

103
00:07:10,387 --> 00:07:12,795
Let's build the actual document now.

104
00:07:13,067 --> 00:07:15,875
The system boundary document is exhibit A.

105
00:07:16,148 --> 00:07:21,765
It's the first file your auditor opens, and it's the map they use to

106
00:07:22,037 --> 00:07:25,247
understand your entire system before fieldwork even starts.

107
00:07:25,519 --> 00:07:31,136
It has a system name, a system description, a version, a document date, and

108
00:07:31,408 --> 00:07:37,025
a document owner, because version control on your compliance documentation is itself evidence that

109
00:07:37,298 --> 00:07:40,909
you're managing your own program, and auditors notice that.

110
00:07:41,181 --> 00:07:46,798
It has our subservice organization disclosure, with the carve out method stated explicitly for

111
00:07:47,070 --> 00:07:51,082
each vendor and the specific evidence backing that carve out.

112
00:07:51,355 --> 00:07:56,972
It has our Trust Service Criteria selection, each one paired with a one sentence

113
00:07:57,244 --> 00:08:00,855
rationale tied to a real obligation, not a vibe.

114
00:08:01,127 --> 00:08:05,540
And then it has the piece that actually does the work.

115
00:08:05,813 --> 00:08:11,430
The trust service criteria mapping, which connects every single criterion to the specific file

116
00:08:11,702 --> 00:08:14,109
in our codebase that implements it.

117
00:08:14,382 --> 00:08:19,999
Security maps to our API key middleware, implementing control C C six point one,

118
00:08:20,271 --> 00:08:23,882
sitting in a specific file in our API layer.

119
00:08:24,154 --> 00:08:29,771
It maps to Vault's dynamic database credentials, also under C C six point one,

120
00:08:30,044 --> 00:08:32,451
defined in our infrastructure as code.

121
00:08:32,723 --> 00:08:38,340
It maps to our Cilium zero trust network policies, same control, different implementation, because

122
00:08:38,612 --> 00:08:43,828
multiple controls can and should map to the same criterion from different angles.

123
00:08:44,100 --> 00:08:49,717
It maps to R D S encryption at rest under C C six point

124
00:08:49,990 --> 00:08:55,607
six, and TLS one point three on our load balancer under C C six

125
00:08:55,879 --> 00:08:56,779
point seven.

126
00:08:57,051 --> 00:08:59,459
This mapping is the treasure map.

127
00:08:59,731 --> 00:09:05,348
The control is the X, the file is the location, the code is what's

128
00:09:05,620 --> 00:09:06,824
actually buried there.

129
00:09:07,096 --> 00:09:11,108
Validate this file the way you'd validate any infrastructure config.

130
00:09:11,381 --> 00:09:14,189
Parse it, don't just eyeball the indentation.

131
00:09:14,462 --> 00:09:20,078
Commit it to Git, with a message that says exactly what it is, because

132
00:09:20,351 --> 00:09:25,165
your commit history itself becomes part of your change management evidence later.

133
00:09:25,438 --> 00:09:31,055
This one document, done properly, with the subservice organization disclosure included from day one,

134
00:09:31,327 --> 00:09:36,944
saves you weeks of scrambling later when an auditor asks a question you should

135
00:09:37,216 --> 00:09:39,222
have already answered in writing.

136
00:09:39,495 --> 00:09:45,112
Coming up next, we tackle scope, and I'm going to show you the single

137
00:09:45,384 --> 00:09:49,797
most common mistake that costs teams weeks of wasted evidence collection.

138
00:09:50,070 --> 00:09:54,884
Scoping something into your audit that nobody actually asked you to include.

139
00:09:55,157 --> 00:10:00,773
Let's make the management assertion concept completely concrete, because it's the piece that unlocks

140
00:10:01,046 --> 00:10:04,255
everything else about how these audits actually work.

141
00:10:04,528 --> 00:10:10,145
Picture your CTO sitting down and signing a document that says, in plain language,

142
00:10:10,417 --> 00:10:16,034
here is our system, here are the controls we have built around it, and

143
00:10:16,306 --> 00:10:20,720
here is our claim about how well those controls actually work.

144
00:10:20,992 --> 00:10:26,609
That signed claim is the management assertion, and it is the actual subject of

145
00:10:26,881 --> 00:10:27,781
the audit.

146
00:10:28,054 --> 00:10:33,671
The auditor is not wandering through your Kubernetes cluster forming their own independent opinion

147
00:10:33,943 --> 00:10:34,843
from scratch.

148
00:10:35,115 --> 00:10:40,732
They are testing a specific claim your own leadership already made, in writing, before

149
00:10:41,005 --> 00:10:43,011
the auditor ever showed up.

150
00:10:43,283 --> 00:10:48,900
This matters enormously for how you prepare, because it means the system boundary document

151
00:10:49,172 --> 00:10:54,789
you're building right now isn't just documentation for your own team's benefit, it's the

152
00:10:55,062 --> 00:11:00,678
first draft of exactly what your CTO is going to sign their name to

153
00:11:00,951 --> 00:11:01,851
later.

154
00:11:02,123 --> 00:11:07,740
Let's also talk about why the Trust Service Criteria approach, without a fixed checklist,

155
00:11:08,013 --> 00:11:13,629
is actually the harder path in the short term but the better path in

156
00:11:13,902 --> 00:11:15,105
the long term.

157
00:11:15,378 --> 00:11:20,995
A fixed checklist framework tells you exactly what sixty four things to implement, and

158
00:11:21,267 --> 00:11:26,884
you implement them, and you're done, regardless of whether any of those sixty four

159
00:11:27,156 --> 00:11:30,767
things are actually relevant to what your system does.

160
00:11:31,040 --> 00:11:33,848
SOC two forces you to think instead.

161
00:11:34,120 --> 00:11:39,737
It asks, given what you specifically built, what controls does the risk profile of

162
00:11:40,010 --> 00:11:42,016
this specific system actually require.

163
00:11:42,288 --> 00:11:43,893
That's harder up front.

164
00:11:44,165 --> 00:11:49,782
It's also exactly why a well built SOC two program tends to produce a

165
00:11:50,055 --> 00:11:55,671
genuinely more secure system than a checklist compliance exercise, because you were forced to

166
00:11:55,944 --> 00:12:01,561
reason about your own actual risk the entire way through, rather than pattern matching

167
00:12:01,833 --> 00:12:03,839
against somebody else's generic template.

168
00:12:04,112 --> 00:12:09,728
One more thing worth nailing down before we move to scope in the next

169
00:12:10,001 --> 00:12:10,901
lecture.

170
00:12:11,173 --> 00:12:16,790
Notice that our system boundary document isn't a static artifact you write once and

171
00:12:17,063 --> 00:12:17,963
forget.

172
00:12:18,235 --> 00:12:23,852
Every time you add a new subservice organization, every time you pick up a

173
00:12:24,124 --> 00:12:29,741
new criterion because a new customer contract adds an availability SLA you didn't have

174
00:12:30,013 --> 00:12:35,630
before, this document changes, and that change history itself is evidence of an actively

175
00:12:35,903 --> 00:12:41,520
managed compliance program rather than a one time box ticking exercise performed the week

176
00:12:41,792 --> 00:12:42,996
before an audit.

177
00:12:43,268 --> 00:12:48,885
Auditors can tell the difference between a document that's been living and breathing in

178
00:12:49,157 --> 00:12:54,774
your Git history for a year versus one that was authored three days before

179
00:12:55,047 --> 00:12:56,250
the engagement started.

180
00:12:56,523 --> 00:13:02,139
Build the habit of updating it now, and it pays for itself every single

181
00:13:02,412 --> 00:13:05,622
audit cycle going forward, not just this one.

182
00:13:05,894 --> 00:13:11,511
Let's talk briefly about who actually reads a SOC two report once it exists,

183
00:13:11,783 --> 00:13:16,196
because understanding the audience changes how you write the underlying documents.

184
00:13:16,469 --> 00:13:22,086
It is almost never read cover to cover by a curious engineer on the

185
00:13:22,358 --> 00:13:23,258
customer's side.

186
00:13:23,531 --> 00:13:29,147
It is read by a security reviewer on a procurement team, working through a

187
00:13:29,420 --> 00:13:35,037
checklist of their own, cross referencing your Trust Service Criteria selection against whatever data

188
00:13:35,309 --> 00:13:38,117
sensitivity tier your specific contract falls into.

189
00:13:38,390 --> 00:13:44,007
If you're selling into healthcare or financial services specifically, that reviewer is looking for

190
00:13:44,279 --> 00:13:49,896
Confidentiality and Processing Integrity in particular, and if those two criteria aren't clearly justified

191
00:13:50,168 --> 00:13:55,785
in your system description, expect a follow up question before the deal even reaches

192
00:13:56,058 --> 00:13:56,958
legal.

193
00:13:57,230 --> 00:14:02,847
Writing your system boundary document with that specific reader in mind, not an abstract

194
00:14:03,119 --> 00:14:08,736
auditor, changes what you choose to spell out explicitly versus what you leave implicit.

195
00:14:09,009 --> 00:14:13,021
And one final grounding point before we move into scope.

196
00:14:13,293 --> 00:14:18,910
Everything in this course maps to a real, running system, not a hypothetical one,

197
00:14:19,182 --> 00:14:24,799
which matters because the details you're about to see, real file paths, real Terraform

198
00:14:25,072 --> 00:14:30,688
resources, real control identifiers, are the actual difference between a course that teaches you

199
00:14:30,961 --> 00:14:36,578
the shape of SOC two and one that hands you something you can literally

200
00:14:36,850 --> 00:14:40,862
copy, adapt, and run against your own infrastructure starting today.

201
00:14:41,135 --> 00:14:46,751
Keep that distinction in mind as we move faster through the next few lectures,

202
00:14:47,024 --> 00:14:52,641
because the goal from here forward is not just understanding the concepts, it's walking

203
00:14:52,913 --> 00:14:57,728
away with artifacts you can commit to your own repository this week.

204
00:14:59,728 --> 00:15:03,228
LECTURE 2 — SCOPE AND DATA FLOW MAPPING

205
00:15:04,228 --> 00:15:10,122
Scope is the decision that either saves you six weeks or costs you six

206
00:15:10,397 --> 00:15:15,449
weeks, and there is no partial credit for getting it approximately right.

207
00:15:15,725 --> 00:15:20,356
The principle is simple to state and genuinely hard to apply.

208
00:15:20,631 --> 00:15:23,999
Include everything required to deliver your service commitments.

209
00:15:24,274 --> 00:15:27,221
Exclude everything customers do not depend on.

210
00:15:27,497 --> 00:15:33,391
Scope too wide, and you spend a month collecting evidence for systems nobody will

211
00:15:33,666 --> 00:15:34,929
ever ask about.

212
00:15:35,205 --> 00:15:41,099
Scope too narrow, and the auditor, not you, decides where the line should have

213
00:15:41,374 --> 00:15:47,268
been, in the form of a finding, after fieldwork has already started, which is

214
00:15:47,544 --> 00:15:50,912
the worst possible time to have that conversation.

215
00:15:51,187 --> 00:15:55,397
Here's a pattern I see constantly with early stage companies.

216
00:15:55,673 --> 00:16:01,567
A team scopes their entire staging environment into the audit boundary, because, in their

217
00:16:01,842 --> 00:16:04,368
words, it's part of our infrastructure.

218
00:16:04,643 --> 00:16:10,538
Three weeks later they're producing evidence for a system that has never once touched

219
00:16:10,813 --> 00:16:16,286
customer data, and the auditor is asking, quite reasonably, why is this here.

220
00:16:16,561 --> 00:16:18,245
The fix isn't cleverness.

221
00:16:18,521 --> 00:16:24,415
It's writing the boundary down, explicitly, before fieldwork begins, in a document your auditor

222
00:16:24,690 --> 00:16:30,584
reviews on day one, so the conversation about what's in and what's out happens

223
00:16:30,860 --> 00:16:32,965
on your terms, not theirs.

224
00:16:33,240 --> 00:16:37,450
Let's define the actual boundary for the Financial RAG Agent.

225
00:16:37,726 --> 00:16:42,778
In scope, we have the E K S cluster running the application.

226
00:16:43,053 --> 00:16:46,421
R D S PostgreSQL with the pgvector extension.

227
00:16:46,696 --> 00:16:48,381
ElastiCache Redis for caching.

228
00:16:48,656 --> 00:16:51,603
S3 buckets for backups and ingestion artifacts.

229
00:16:51,878 --> 00:16:54,404
The E C R container registry.

230
00:16:54,680 --> 00:16:56,785
The FastAPI application service itself.

231
00:16:57,060 --> 00:17:00,007
The agent workers that process customer queries.

232
00:17:00,282 --> 00:17:04,072
The ingestion cron jobs that build our knowledge base.

233
00:17:04,347 --> 00:17:06,031
Vault for credential management.

234
00:17:06,306 --> 00:17:08,411
Cilium for network policy enforcement.

235
00:17:08,687 --> 00:17:10,792
Falco for runtime security monitoring.

236
00:17:11,067 --> 00:17:12,751
ArgoCD for GitOps deployment.

237
00:17:13,026 --> 00:17:17,237
And our full observability stack, Prometheus, Loki, Tempo, and Grafana.

238
00:17:17,512 --> 00:17:23,406
Everything here either touches production customer data directly or supports the production service in

239
00:17:23,681 --> 00:17:27,891
a way that would break something if it went down.

240
00:17:28,167 --> 00:17:29,430
That's the test.

241
00:17:29,705 --> 00:17:35,599
Out of scope, and this is where you need a real justification for each

242
00:17:35,875 --> 00:17:39,243
exclusion, not just a wave of the hand.

243
00:17:39,518 --> 00:17:45,412
Developer workstations are out, covered by your endpoint policy, and you should reference that

244
00:17:45,688 --> 00:17:49,477
policy by name, not just assert it's handled somewhere.

245
00:17:49,752 --> 00:17:55,646
Customer on premises environments are out, that's the customer's own responsibility, and it belongs

246
00:17:55,922 --> 00:17:57,606
in your responsibility matrix.

247
00:17:57,881 --> 00:18:03,775
OpenAI and Groq A P Is are out, covered by the vendor assessment and

248
00:18:04,050 --> 00:18:08,260
subservice organization treatment we set up in the previous lecture.

249
00:18:08,536 --> 00:18:14,430
The public internet between the customer and our load balancer is out, by definition,

250
00:18:14,705 --> 00:18:18,494
because our control boundary literally starts at TLS termination.

251
00:18:18,770 --> 00:18:24,664
And staging and development clusters are out, but only if you can actually prove

252
00:18:24,939 --> 00:18:27,465
no customer data ever flows there.

253
00:18:27,741 --> 00:18:33,635
If your ingestion pipeline has ever restored a production snapshot into staging for debugging,

254
00:18:33,910 --> 00:18:39,804
that's now a data flow you have to document and defend, or a practice

255
00:18:40,080 --> 00:18:44,711
you need to fix before you can honestly make this claim.

256
00:18:44,986 --> 00:18:50,880
Now here's a concept that most SOC two explainers skip entirely, and it matters

257
00:18:51,156 --> 00:18:54,524
more than half the things they do cover.

258
00:18:54,799 --> 00:18:57,325
Complementary User Entity Controls, or CUECs.

259
00:18:57,600 --> 00:19:03,495
Some of your controls only actually work if your customer does something on their

260
00:19:03,770 --> 00:19:04,670
side too.

261
00:19:04,945 --> 00:19:10,839
If a customer manages their own API key rotation internally, or is responsible for

262
00:19:11,115 --> 00:19:17,009
restricting who on their team can call your API, that's a CUEC, a control

263
00:19:17,284 --> 00:19:23,178
your report explicitly states the customer must implement for the overall objective to actually

264
00:19:23,454 --> 00:19:24,354
hold.

265
00:19:24,629 --> 00:19:30,523
Miss this distinction, and your Type Two report ends up claiming a control works

266
00:19:30,798 --> 00:19:36,693
when it secretly depends on a customer behavior you can't observe and definitely can't

267
00:19:36,968 --> 00:19:37,868
enforce.

268
00:19:38,143 --> 00:19:41,090
A good auditor will ask you directly.

269
00:19:41,366 --> 00:19:47,260
What do you expect the user entity to do, that you're not doing yourself.

270
00:19:47,535 --> 00:19:53,429
Have that answer ready, in writing, in your trust boundary document, before they ask.

271
00:19:53,705 --> 00:19:56,652
Let's build the trust boundary document itself.

272
00:19:56,927 --> 00:20:02,821
We're drawing this as an actual architecture diagram, because auditors genuinely prefer something they

273
00:20:03,097 --> 00:20:08,991
can diff in Git over a Visio export that lives on someone's desktop and

274
00:20:09,266 --> 00:20:10,529
never gets updated.

275
00:20:10,804 --> 00:20:15,857
Internet flows into our A L B over TLS one point three.

276
00:20:16,132 --> 00:20:22,026
The A L B talks to our E K S cluster over Cilium's mutual

277
00:20:22,301 --> 00:20:23,201
TLS.

278
00:20:23,477 --> 00:20:29,371
Inside the cluster, our FastAPI service is customer facing, our agent workers process the

279
00:20:29,646 --> 00:20:35,540
actual queries, our ingestion cron jobs build the knowledge base, our observability stack watches

280
00:20:35,816 --> 00:20:40,868
everything, Vault manages every secret, and ArgoCD deploys every change from Git.

281
00:20:41,143 --> 00:20:47,037
From there, TLS and mutual TLS carry traffic to R D S PostgreSQL as

282
00:20:47,313 --> 00:20:53,207
our primary store, to ElastiCache Redis as our cache, and to S3 for backups

283
00:20:53,482 --> 00:20:54,382
and artifacts.

284
00:20:54,658 --> 00:20:58,026
And then, separately, our out of scope boundary.

285
00:20:58,301 --> 00:21:04,195
OpenAI's A P I, covered by their own SOC two Type Two and our

286
00:21:04,470 --> 00:21:06,154
executed Data Processing Addendum.

287
00:21:06,430 --> 00:21:12,324
Groq's A P I, covered by their security questionnaire and D P A while

288
00:21:12,599 --> 00:21:15,125
we wait on their own attestation.

289
00:21:15,401 --> 00:21:20,874
SEC EDGAR, a public data source where no customer data is ever shared.

290
00:21:21,149 --> 00:21:27,043
And AWS global infrastructure, covered by the AWS Shared Responsibility Model and their own

291
00:21:27,319 --> 00:21:30,687
SOC two Type Two, accessible through AWS Artifact.

292
00:21:30,962 --> 00:21:36,856
The single most valuable table in this entire lecture is the control at boundary

293
00:21:37,132 --> 00:21:39,658
table, so let's build it properly.

294
00:21:39,933 --> 00:21:45,827
Internet to A L B, inbound, TLS one point three only, evidenced by our

295
00:21:46,102 --> 00:21:49,050
ingress annotations and a live openssl verification.

296
00:21:49,325 --> 00:21:55,219
A L B to E K S pods, inbound, A P I key validation,

297
00:21:55,494 --> 00:21:57,599
evidenced by our middleware logs.

298
00:21:57,875 --> 00:22:03,769
Pod to pod, internal, Cilium mutual TLS plus network policy, evidenced by a live

299
00:22:04,044 --> 00:22:04,944
policy query.

300
00:22:05,220 --> 00:22:11,114
Pod to R D S, internal, TLS verify full plus Vault's dynamic credentials, evidenced

301
00:22:11,389 --> 00:22:13,494
by PostgreSQL's own SSL status.

302
00:22:13,769 --> 00:22:19,664
Pod to Redis, internal, TLS plus a Redis auth token, evidenced by a TLS

303
00:22:19,939 --> 00:22:21,623
enabled redis client check.

304
00:22:21,898 --> 00:22:27,792
Pod to S3, internal, I A M roles for service accounts plus a VPC

305
00:22:28,068 --> 00:22:32,699
endpoint, evidenced by I A M policy and S3 access logs.

306
00:22:32,974 --> 00:22:38,868
And pod to OpenAI, outbound, TLS one point three plus our D P A

307
00:22:39,144 --> 00:22:45,038
and a zero retention configuration, evidenced by our A P I client's own connection

308
00:22:45,313 --> 00:22:46,213
settings.

309
00:22:46,489 --> 00:22:52,383
Now let's map the actual data flows, because this is where auditors find the

310
00:22:52,658 --> 00:22:54,342
surprises nobody planned for.

311
00:22:54,617 --> 00:23:00,512
If you don't know exactly where a piece of data goes, you cannot possibly

312
00:23:00,787 --> 00:23:04,576
protect it, and vague answers here generate real findings.

313
00:23:04,851 --> 00:23:10,746
Flow D F dash zero zero one is SEC filing ingestion, moving from SEC

314
00:23:11,021 --> 00:23:15,231
EDGAR into our S3 raw bucket and then into pgvector.

315
00:23:15,506 --> 00:23:21,400
It's classified public, because SEC filings are public documents, but it still needs real

316
00:23:21,676 --> 00:23:25,886
integrity controls, because the risk here is accuracy, not secrecy.

317
00:23:26,161 --> 00:23:32,055
We protect it with TLS for the download, SHA two fifty six checksum validation,

318
00:23:32,331 --> 00:23:36,120
and idempotent processing with a file hash based deduplication.

319
00:23:36,395 --> 00:23:42,289
Flow D F dash zero zero two is user query processing, moving from a

320
00:23:42,565 --> 00:23:48,459
customer's A P I client through FastAPI, through pgvector, out to OpenAI, and back

321
00:23:48,734 --> 00:23:49,997
to the customer.

322
00:23:50,272 --> 00:23:56,167
This one is classified confidential, because a user's financial question can genuinely reveal their

323
00:23:56,442 --> 00:23:57,705
own investment strategy.

324
00:23:57,980 --> 00:24:03,874
We protect it with end to end TLS one point three, A P I

325
00:24:04,150 --> 00:24:09,623
key authentication, field level encryption for anything we store, and full audit logging.

326
00:24:09,898 --> 00:24:15,792
Flow D F dash zero zero three is analysis history storage, moving from our

327
00:24:16,068 --> 00:24:20,278
agent workers into a dedicated table in R D S.

328
00:24:20,553 --> 00:24:26,447
Also confidential, and it's our most heavily controlled flow, because it's effectively our audit

329
00:24:26,723 --> 00:24:27,623
trail.

330
00:24:27,898 --> 00:24:33,792
R D S encryption at rest, an append only table where triggers physically block

331
00:24:34,068 --> 00:24:39,962
update and delete statements, and a hash chain across records so any tampering becomes

332
00:24:40,237 --> 00:24:41,137
immediately detectable.

333
00:24:41,412 --> 00:24:47,307
And flow D F dash zero zero four is database credential issuance itself, moving

334
00:24:47,582 --> 00:24:53,055
from Vault's database secrets engine into our application pods through a sidecar agent.

335
00:24:53,330 --> 00:24:59,224
This one is classified restricted, our highest tier, because credential compromise here has no

336
00:24:59,500 --> 00:25:00,400
graceful degradation.

337
00:25:00,675 --> 00:25:06,569
Credentials with a one hour time to live, injected only into in memory temp

338
00:25:06,845 --> 00:25:12,739
file systems, never written to disk, and expiring automatically whether or not anyone remembers

339
00:25:13,014 --> 00:25:14,277
to revoke them.

340
00:25:14,553 --> 00:25:15,816
Classification isn't decoration.

341
00:25:16,091 --> 00:25:21,985
Restricted, confidential, internal, public, in that order, each with real consequences for how much

342
00:25:22,260 --> 00:25:24,786
control effort you're required to justify.

343
00:25:25,062 --> 00:25:30,956
Get the classification right, and the rest of your control selection in the next

344
00:25:31,231 --> 00:25:32,915
lecture becomes almost mechanical.

345
00:25:33,191 --> 00:25:39,085
Commit both files, the trust boundary document and the data flow mapping, together, because

346
00:25:39,360 --> 00:25:45,254
they tell one coherent story about where your fence line sits and what crosses

347
00:25:45,530 --> 00:25:46,430
it.

348
00:25:46,705 --> 00:25:52,599
Next lecture, we take everything we've drawn here and turn it into an actual

349
00:25:52,874 --> 00:25:58,769
control catalog, nineteen specific controls, each one tied to a specific line of code.

350
00:25:59,044 --> 00:26:04,938
Let's spend a moment on why data classification, specifically the four tiers we're using

351
00:26:05,213 --> 00:26:11,108
here, restricted, confidential, internal, and public, actually changes your engineering decisions in ways that

352
00:26:11,383 --> 00:26:13,067
matter beyond just labeling.

353
00:26:13,342 --> 00:26:19,236
When you classify something restricted, like a database credential, you're making a statement that

354
00:26:19,512 --> 00:26:25,406
this data has essentially zero tolerance for exposure, which is exactly why our Vault

355
00:26:25,681 --> 00:26:31,575
issued credentials never touch disk at all, they exist purely in memory and expire

356
00:26:31,851 --> 00:26:32,751
automatically.

357
00:26:33,026 --> 00:26:38,920
When you classify something confidential, like a user's query history, you're saying exposure would

358
00:26:39,196 --> 00:26:45,090
cause real harm to a specific person, which justifies field level encryption plus an

359
00:26:45,365 --> 00:26:46,628
audit trail specifically.

360
00:26:46,903 --> 00:26:52,798
Public data, like an SEC filing anyone can download themselves from EDGAR, doesn't need

361
00:26:53,073 --> 00:26:58,967
secrecy controls at all, but it absolutely still needs integrity controls, because the actual

362
00:26:59,242 --> 00:27:05,137
risk with public data usually isn't somebody stealing it, it's somebody being served a

363
00:27:05,412 --> 00:27:08,780
corrupted or tampered version of it without realizing.

364
00:27:09,055 --> 00:27:14,949
Here's a genuinely practical exercise worth doing with your own system before you move

365
00:27:15,225 --> 00:27:16,125
forward.

366
00:27:16,400 --> 00:27:22,294
Take every single data element that flows through your architecture and force yourself to

367
00:27:22,570 --> 00:27:28,464
write down its classification, explicitly, in one sentence, the same way we did for

368
00:27:28,739 --> 00:27:30,002
our four flows.

369
00:27:30,277 --> 00:27:36,172
Most teams discover, doing this exercise for the first time, that they have data

370
00:27:36,447 --> 00:27:42,341
flowing through their system that nobody had ever actually classified at all, it just

371
00:27:42,616 --> 00:27:48,511
existed in some ambiguous state where everyone assumed somebody else had thought about it.

372
00:27:48,786 --> 00:27:54,680
That ambiguity is exactly where audit findings come from, and exactly where real security

373
00:27:54,955 --> 00:28:00,850
incidents come from too, because unclassified data tends to get handled with whatever level

374
00:28:01,125 --> 00:28:07,019
of care happens to be convenient in the moment, rather than the level of

375
00:28:07,294 --> 00:28:08,978
care it actually requires.

376
00:28:09,254 --> 00:28:15,148
And one more layer worth adding to your trust boundary thinking, specifically for anyone

377
00:28:15,423 --> 00:28:18,791
building on Kubernetes the way we are here.

378
00:28:19,067 --> 00:28:24,540
Namespace boundaries are not automatically security boundaries unless you explicitly make them one.

379
00:28:24,815 --> 00:28:30,709
Two workloads sitting in the same namespace, by default, can often reach each other

380
00:28:30,985 --> 00:28:36,879
over the network with zero restriction at all, regardless of how carefully you've drawn

381
00:28:37,154 --> 00:28:39,259
your architecture diagram on paper.

382
00:28:39,535 --> 00:28:45,429
This is exactly why Cilium's network policies aren't a nice to have sitting on

383
00:28:45,704 --> 00:28:51,598
top of our trust boundary diagram, they're the actual mechanism that makes the diagram

384
00:28:51,874 --> 00:28:54,400
true in practice rather than aspirational.

385
00:28:54,675 --> 00:29:00,569
Draw the fence on paper first, always, but then go verify, with a live

386
00:29:00,844 --> 00:29:06,739
policy query, that the fence you drew is the fence that Kubernetes is actually

387
00:29:07,014 --> 00:29:08,277
enforcing at runtime.

388
00:29:08,552 --> 00:29:14,446
Here's a scoping question worth asking about every single component before you finalize your

389
00:29:14,722 --> 00:29:17,669
boundary, and it's simpler than it sounds.

390
00:29:17,944 --> 00:29:23,417
If this component disappeared tonight, would a customer notice within your SLA window.

391
00:29:23,693 --> 00:29:29,587
If yes, it's in scope, full stop, regardless of how you've historically labeled it

392
00:29:29,862 --> 00:29:32,388
internally as production or non production.

393
00:29:32,664 --> 00:29:38,558
If no, and it genuinely never touches customer data even indirectly, it's a candidate

394
00:29:38,833 --> 00:29:44,727
for exclusion, but only once you've actually verified the no touches customer data claim

395
00:29:45,002 --> 00:29:46,687
rather than assumed it.

396
00:29:46,962 --> 00:29:52,856
This single question, applied consistently across your entire architecture inventory, resolves the vast majority

397
00:29:53,131 --> 00:29:59,026
of scoping ambiguity faster than any amount of theoretical discussion about what counts as

398
00:29:59,301 --> 00:30:00,201
infrastructure.

399
00:30:00,476 --> 00:30:06,370
Let's also address a specific edge case teams run into constantly with modern data

400
00:30:06,646 --> 00:30:07,909
platforms like ours.

401
00:30:08,184 --> 00:30:14,078
Your observability stack, Prometheus, Loki, Tempo, Grafana, technically doesn't process customer requests directly, so

402
00:30:14,354 --> 00:30:18,143
it's tempting to wave it out of scope entirely.

403
00:30:18,418 --> 00:30:19,318
Don't.

404
00:30:19,593 --> 00:30:25,487
It absolutely belongs in scope, because it's exactly the system that produces your availability

405
00:30:25,763 --> 00:30:31,657
and monitoring evidence, and an auditor testing your C C seven controls needs to

406
00:30:31,932 --> 00:30:37,405
trust that the monitoring system itself is reliable and hasn't been tampered with.

407
00:30:37,681 --> 00:30:43,575
A monitoring stack that's out of scope but is simultaneously the source of your

408
00:30:43,850 --> 00:30:49,744
evidence for in scope controls creates a genuine logical gap in your own argument,

409
00:30:50,020 --> 00:30:55,914
and a sharp auditor will find that gap in about thirty seconds of reading

410
00:30:56,189 --> 00:30:57,452
your boundary document.

411
00:30:59,452 --> 00:31:02,952
LECTURE 3 — CONTROL SELECTION

412
00:31:03,952 --> 00:31:11,515
Control selection is where most engineers make the same instinctive mistake, and I want

413
00:31:11,807 --> 00:31:16,669
to name it directly before we go any further.

414
00:31:16,961 --> 00:31:21,282
The instinct is, we need all of them.

415
00:31:21,574 --> 00:31:23,195
It feels safe.

416
00:31:23,487 --> 00:31:25,107
It feels thorough.

417
00:31:25,399 --> 00:31:29,721
It is completely wrong, and here's exactly why.

418
00:31:30,012 --> 00:31:37,575
Every control you claim is something you now have to prove, forever, with evidence,

419
00:31:37,867 --> 00:31:43,269
in every single future audit cycle, not just this one.

420
00:31:43,561 --> 00:31:51,124
A control you don't actually need is pure maintenance burden with zero security benefit.

421
00:31:51,416 --> 00:31:58,979
The real discipline, the thing that separates senior engineering from checkbox compliance, is this.

422
00:31:59,271 --> 00:32:06,834
For every control you add, you name the specific criterion and the specific data

423
00:32:07,125 --> 00:32:09,286
flow that requires it.

424
00:32:09,578 --> 00:32:13,359
If you can't name both, cut it.

425
00:32:13,651 --> 00:32:21,214
Let's talk about the control hierarchy precisely, because vague category names cause real confusion

426
00:32:21,506 --> 00:32:22,406
later.

427
00:32:22,698 --> 00:32:30,261
C C six is logical and physical access controls, and for a technical SaaS

428
00:32:30,552 --> 00:32:38,115
company, it's genuinely the largest category, covering how you control who can get into

429
00:32:38,407 --> 00:32:41,108
your system and your data.

430
00:32:41,400 --> 00:32:48,963
C C seven is system operations, monitoring, incident detection, backup, and disaster recovery, the

431
00:32:49,255 --> 00:32:54,117
controls that prove you're actually watching your own system.

432
00:32:54,408 --> 00:33:01,971
C C eight is change management, how you ensure changes are authorized, tested, and

433
00:33:02,263 --> 00:33:08,746
tracked, where GitOps plus branch protection does most of the heavy lifting.

434
00:33:09,037 --> 00:33:16,600
And C C nine is risk mitigation, vendor risk management and business continuity, where

435
00:33:16,892 --> 00:33:19,593
your vendor assessment program lives.

436
00:33:19,885 --> 00:33:27,448
Now, here's a problem that almost every course on this topic completely ignores, and

437
00:33:27,740 --> 00:33:34,763
it's one of the most common findings I see for early stage companies.

438
00:33:35,054 --> 00:33:36,675
Segregation of duties.

439
00:33:36,967 --> 00:33:44,530
C C eight point one, change management, typically wants a pattern where the person

440
00:33:44,821 --> 00:33:52,384
who wrote the code, the person who approves it, and the person who deploys

441
00:33:52,676 --> 00:33:55,377
it, are three different humans.

442
00:33:55,669 --> 00:34:03,232
For a three to five person engineering team, this is genuinely hard, because those

443
00:34:03,524 --> 00:34:11,087
roles keep collapsing onto the same two or three people no matter how you

444
00:34:11,379 --> 00:34:13,539
organize the org chart.

445
00:34:13,831 --> 00:34:21,394
And pretending this problem away doesn't work, because auditors literally check git blame against

446
00:34:21,686 --> 00:34:23,847
your list of approvers.

447
00:34:24,138 --> 00:34:27,380
There are two honest paths here.

448
00:34:27,672 --> 00:34:35,235
One, you accept a documented compensating control, something like every production deploy becomes automatically

449
00:34:35,526 --> 00:34:43,089
visible in a dedicated Slack channel, with a mandatory cooling period before certain higher

450
00:34:43,381 --> 00:34:50,944
risk change types go live, giving you a detective control where you're missing a

451
00:34:51,236 --> 00:34:52,316
preventive one.

452
00:34:52,608 --> 00:35:00,171
Or two, you bring in a fractional or contractor reviewer specifically for security touching

453
00:35:00,463 --> 00:35:08,026
changes, which, by the way, is a completely legitimate service a consultancy can sell,

454
00:35:08,317 --> 00:35:12,639
not just a compliance footnote to apologize for.

455
00:35:12,931 --> 00:35:20,494
Let's build the actual catalog, nineteen controls, each one code linked, each one with

456
00:35:20,786 --> 00:35:25,107
a named owner and an automated evidence source.

457
00:35:25,399 --> 00:35:29,721
Under C C six, logical and physical access.

458
00:35:30,013 --> 00:35:37,576
C C six point one, logical access security software, implemented as A P I

459
00:35:37,867 --> 00:35:45,430
key middleware plus Vault's role based access control plus Kubernetes R B A C,

460
00:35:45,722 --> 00:35:51,124
evidenced daily by querying our cluster's roles and role bindings.

461
00:35:51,416 --> 00:35:58,979
C C six point two, authenticating entities before issuing credentials, implemented as A P

462
00:35:59,271 --> 00:36:06,834
I key provisioning that requires explicit admin approval, evidenced monthly by an A P

463
00:36:07,125 --> 00:36:08,746
I key audit.

464
00:36:09,038 --> 00:36:16,601
C C six point three, removing access on termination, implemented as an A P

465
00:36:16,893 --> 00:36:24,456
I key revocation endpoint with immediate effect, no grace period, no delay, evidenced quarterly

466
00:36:24,747 --> 00:36:27,448
by a full access review.

467
00:36:27,740 --> 00:36:35,303
C C six point six, encryption of data at rest, implemented across four layers,

468
00:36:35,595 --> 00:36:43,158
R D S with a customer managed K M S key, S3 with server

469
00:36:43,450 --> 00:36:51,013
side encryption, E B S volume encryption, and field level encryption for personally identifiable

470
00:36:51,304 --> 00:36:58,867
information inside the database itself, evidenced daily by querying R D S for its

471
00:36:59,159 --> 00:37:00,240
encryption status.

472
00:37:00,531 --> 00:37:08,094
C C six point seven, encryption of data in transit, implemented as TLS one

473
00:37:08,386 --> 00:37:15,949
point three on our load balancer, mutual TLS between pods through Cilium, and TLS

474
00:37:16,241 --> 00:37:23,804
to both R D S and Redis, evidenced daily by a live openssl handshake

475
00:37:24,096 --> 00:37:24,996
test.

476
00:37:25,287 --> 00:37:32,850
And C C six point eight, controls that prevent or detect unauthorized software, implemented

477
00:37:33,142 --> 00:37:40,705
as Falco runtime security, Trivy container scanning in our C I pipeline, and cryptographic

478
00:37:40,997 --> 00:37:46,399
image signing, evidenced daily by scanning our Falco alert logs.

479
00:37:46,691 --> 00:37:49,932
Under C C seven, system operations.

480
00:37:50,224 --> 00:37:57,787
C C seven point one, availability and performance, implemented as Prometheus S L O

481
00:37:58,078 --> 00:38:05,641
tracking, PagerDuty on call escalation, horizontal pod autoscaling, and Karpenter node provisioning, evidenced daily

482
00:38:05,933 --> 00:38:09,715
through our Grafana S L O dashboard.

483
00:38:10,006 --> 00:38:17,569
C C seven point two, monitoring of security events, implemented as Falco alerts routed

484
00:38:17,861 --> 00:38:25,424
through Falcosidekick into PagerDuty, Prometheus AlertManager for metric based alerts, and AWS GuardDuty for

485
00:38:25,716 --> 00:38:27,337
cloud level monitoring.

486
00:38:27,628 --> 00:38:35,191
C C seven point four, backup and recovery, implemented as daily Velero backups, thirty

487
00:38:35,483 --> 00:38:43,046
day R D S snapshot retention, and quarterly restore tests that actually verify we

488
00:38:43,338 --> 00:38:48,740
can recover, not just that a backup file exists somewhere.

489
00:38:49,032 --> 00:38:56,595
And C C seven point five, responding to identified security incidents, implemented as version

490
00:38:56,886 --> 00:39:04,449
controlled incident runbooks, PagerDuty escalation, and a mandatory post incident review after every single

491
00:39:04,741 --> 00:39:05,641
event.

492
00:39:05,933 --> 00:39:09,174
Under C C eight, change management.

493
00:39:09,466 --> 00:39:17,029
C C eight point one, implemented as GitHub branch protection requiring two reviewers, ArgoCD

494
00:39:17,321 --> 00:39:24,884
managing all cluster state from Git, and canary deployments to limit blast radius, with

495
00:39:25,176 --> 00:39:32,738
our segregation of duties compensating control documented explicitly for the periods where team size

496
00:39:33,030 --> 00:39:36,272
makes the ideal pattern genuinely impossible.

497
00:39:36,563 --> 00:39:39,805
Under C C nine, risk mitigation.

498
00:39:40,096 --> 00:39:47,659
C C nine point two, vendor risk management, implemented as annual vendor assessments, review

499
00:39:47,951 --> 00:39:55,514
of subservice organization SOC two reports, executed Data Processing Addendums, and our carve out

500
00:39:55,806 --> 00:39:57,967
documentation from Lecture One.

501
00:39:58,258 --> 00:40:03,661
And then our four optional criteria layer directly on top.

502
00:40:03,952 --> 00:40:11,515
A one point one, availability commitments communicated, our ninety nine point nine percent S

503
00:40:11,807 --> 00:40:16,669
L A documented and visible on a live dashboard.

504
00:40:16,961 --> 00:40:23,983
A one point two, environmental protections, multi A Z deployment and automatic failover.

505
00:40:24,275 --> 00:40:31,838
P I one point one, processing integrity, our SHA two fifty six deduplication and

506
00:40:32,130 --> 00:40:35,371
A C I D compliant transactions.

507
00:40:35,663 --> 00:40:43,226
C one point one and C one point two, confidentiality during collection and during

508
00:40:43,518 --> 00:40:47,839
disposal, field encryption and a documented purge process.

509
00:40:48,131 --> 00:40:55,694
P one point one and P four point two, privacy notice communication and retention

510
00:40:55,986 --> 00:41:03,549
and disposal policy, our thirty day query retention and a user facing deletion endpoint.

511
00:41:03,841 --> 00:41:11,404
Notice something important here, because it's the actual design insight, not just a catalog

512
00:41:11,695 --> 00:41:12,595
fact.

513
00:41:12,887 --> 00:41:20,450
Our field encryption module satisfies both C C six point six and C one

514
00:41:20,742 --> 00:41:22,363
point one simultaneously.

515
00:41:22,654 --> 00:41:28,057
That's not a coincidence, and it's not just efficient bookkeeping.

516
00:41:28,348 --> 00:41:31,049
It's the real design goal.

517
00:41:31,341 --> 00:41:38,904
A control implemented well in code usually satisfies more than one criterion at once,

518
00:41:39,196 --> 00:41:46,759
which is exactly why designing for compliance from day one is dramatically cheaper than

519
00:41:47,051 --> 00:41:54,614
retrofitting it eighteen months later, after you've already signed your first enterprise contract and

520
00:41:54,905 --> 00:41:59,227
the customer's legal team is asking pointed questions.

521
00:41:59,519 --> 00:42:07,082
Nineteen controls, every single one linked to a real file, a real owner, and

522
00:42:07,374 --> 00:42:10,075
a real automated evidence source.

523
00:42:10,366 --> 00:42:11,987
Commit this file.

524
00:42:12,279 --> 00:42:19,842
Next lecture, we take this catalog and we automate the proof, because a control

525
00:42:20,134 --> 00:42:27,697
without contemporaneous evidence is just a claim, and claims are exactly what get challenged

526
00:42:27,988 --> 00:42:29,069
in fieldwork.

527
00:42:29,360 --> 00:42:36,923
Let's go a layer deeper on why control mapping to code, specifically, rather than

528
00:42:37,215 --> 00:42:44,778
control mapping to a policy document, is the actual differentiator between a compliance program

529
00:42:45,070 --> 00:42:52,633
that survives a Type Two audit and one that quietly falls apart the first

530
00:42:52,925 --> 00:42:57,246
time an auditor asks a follow up question.

531
00:42:57,538 --> 00:43:04,561
A policy document that says we encrypt data at rest is a claim.

532
00:43:04,853 --> 00:43:12,416
A Terraform resource block that provisions a KMS key and attaches it to your

533
00:43:12,707 --> 00:43:20,270
RDS instance is proof, because it either exists in your infrastructure or it doesn't,

534
00:43:20,562 --> 00:43:28,125
and there's no interpretation gap between what the document says and what's actually running

535
00:43:28,417 --> 00:43:29,497
in production.

536
00:43:29,789 --> 00:43:37,352
Every single control in our catalog is deliberately anchored to a specific file, a

537
00:43:37,644 --> 00:43:45,207
specific class, a specific method, precisely so that the gap between claim and reality

538
00:43:45,499 --> 00:43:50,901
can never quietly widen without somebody noticing in code review.

539
00:43:51,192 --> 00:43:58,755
Let's also talk honestly about control fatigue, because it's a real failure mode for

540
00:43:59,047 --> 00:44:03,909
growing teams that nobody warns you about early enough.

541
00:44:04,201 --> 00:44:11,764
As your system grows, the number of controls you're formally tracking grows too, and

542
00:44:12,056 --> 00:44:19,619
at some point, without deliberate maintenance, your control catalog starts drifting away from what

543
00:44:19,910 --> 00:44:22,071
your system actually does.

544
00:44:22,363 --> 00:44:29,926
A control might reference a file that got refactored eighteen months ago and moved

545
00:44:30,218 --> 00:44:31,838
somewhere else entirely.

546
00:44:32,130 --> 00:44:36,452
The owner listed might have left the company.

547
00:44:36,744 --> 00:44:42,686
The automated evidence command might reference a tool you've since replaced.

548
00:44:42,978 --> 00:44:50,541
This is exactly why the owner field on every control isn't bureaucratic overhead, it's

549
00:44:50,832 --> 00:44:58,395
a living pointer to a specific human whose job includes noticing when their control's

550
00:44:58,687 --> 00:45:06,250
implementation has drifted, and updating the catalog before an auditor discovers the drift for

551
00:45:06,542 --> 00:45:08,163
you during fieldwork.

552
00:45:08,454 --> 00:45:11,696
One more genuinely useful habit here.

553
00:45:11,987 --> 00:45:19,550
Whenever you ship a meaningful architecture change, ask explicitly, in the pull request itself,

554
00:45:19,842 --> 00:45:24,704
does this change touch any control in our catalog.

555
00:45:24,996 --> 00:45:32,559
Teams that build this question into their normal PR template, right alongside the usual

556
00:45:32,851 --> 00:45:40,414
does this need a migration or does this need a runbook update, tend to

557
00:45:40,705 --> 00:45:48,268
walk into their Type Two renewal with a control catalog that's still accurate, rather

558
00:45:48,560 --> 00:45:56,123
than discovering during fieldwork that half their evidence commands are now pointing at files

559
00:45:56,415 --> 00:45:58,576
that no longer exist.

560
00:45:58,867 --> 00:46:06,430
That single habit, treated as seriously as any other engineering discipline, is worth more

561
00:46:06,722 --> 00:46:12,124
than almost any other single practice in this entire lecture.

562
00:46:12,416 --> 00:46:19,979
Let's talk about ordering, specifically which controls to implement first if you're starting completely

563
00:46:20,271 --> 00:46:27,834
from zero rather than retrofitting an existing system the way we're doing here with

564
00:46:28,126 --> 00:46:30,286
an already built platform.

565
00:46:30,578 --> 00:46:38,141
Access controls first, always, because C C six failures are the single most common

566
00:46:38,433 --> 00:46:45,996
Type One finding across the industry, and they're also usually the cheapest to fix,

567
00:46:46,288 --> 00:46:53,851
an API key middleware and role based access control policy can genuinely be built

568
00:46:54,142 --> 00:46:57,924
in a single sprint by one engineer.

569
00:46:58,216 --> 00:47:05,779
Encryption second, because it's largely infrastructure configuration rather than new application logic, a KMS

570
00:47:06,070 --> 00:47:10,932
key and a Terraform flag flip, not a redesign.

571
00:47:11,224 --> 00:47:18,787
Change management third, because it requires organizational buy in around branch protection and review

572
00:47:19,079 --> 00:47:26,642
requirements, which takes longer to actually stick as a habit than it takes to

573
00:47:26,934 --> 00:47:29,635
configure in GitHub's settings panel.

574
00:47:29,926 --> 00:47:37,489
And monitoring and backup last in this rough sequence, not because they matter less,

575
00:47:37,781 --> 00:47:45,344
but because they benefit from having a stable, access controlled, encrypted system already in

576
00:47:45,636 --> 00:47:51,578
place to actually monitor and back up in the first place.

577
00:47:51,870 --> 00:47:56,732
One more genuinely underappreciated point about control ownership specifically.

578
00:47:57,024 --> 00:48:04,587
Assign owners by function, not by individual name, wherever your team size allows it.

579
00:48:04,878 --> 00:48:12,441
At sign security team owns CC6.8 reads very differently in your control catalog eighteen

580
00:48:12,733 --> 00:48:20,296
months from now than at Jamie owns CC6.8, because Jamie leaves companies, teams reorganize,

581
00:48:20,588 --> 00:48:28,151
and a control catalog anchored to individual names quietly becomes stale the moment any

582
00:48:28,443 --> 00:48:30,603
single person changes roles.

583
00:48:30,895 --> 00:48:38,458
Anchor ownership to the function that will always exist in some form, security team,

584
00:48:38,750 --> 00:48:46,313
platform team, compliance team, and let whoever currently holds that function inherit the responsibility

585
00:48:46,605 --> 00:48:54,168
automatically, without anyone needing to remember to update nineteen separate YAML entries the next

586
00:48:54,459 --> 00:48:57,160
time your org chart shifts.

587
00:48:59,160 --> 00:49:02,660
LECTURE 4 — EVIDENCE AUTOMATION

588
00:49:03,660 --> 00:49:12,333
Here's the uncomfortable truth about SOC two audits that almost nobody says out loud

589
00:49:12,624 --> 00:49:15,102
until it's too late.

590
00:49:15,393 --> 00:49:19,109
Controls almost never fail an audit.

591
00:49:19,401 --> 00:49:20,640
Evidence does.

592
00:49:20,931 --> 00:49:29,603
The finding you actually see, again and again, reads something like, evidence was not

593
00:49:29,894 --> 00:49:31,133
collected contemporaneously.

594
00:49:31,424 --> 00:49:40,096
What that really means is somebody reconstructed proof after the fact from a screenshot,

595
00:49:40,388 --> 00:49:49,060
and a screenshot from memory is always weaker, in an auditor's eyes, than a

596
00:49:49,351 --> 00:49:55,545
system generated log with a real timestamp attached to it.

597
00:49:55,837 --> 00:50:00,792
The fix isn't more discipline from your team.

598
00:50:01,083 --> 00:50:07,897
It's automation that runs whether or not anyone remembers it exists.

599
00:50:08,188 --> 00:50:09,088
Nightly.

600
00:50:09,380 --> 00:50:10,280
Unattended.

601
00:50:10,571 --> 00:50:11,471
Immutable.

602
00:50:11,762 --> 00:50:14,859
Let's build the actual pipeline.

603
00:50:15,150 --> 00:50:23,823
A GitHub Actions workflow, triggered on a cron schedule at midnight U T C

604
00:50:24,114 --> 00:50:32,786
every single day, plus a manual dispatch trigger for whenever you need evidence on

605
00:50:33,077 --> 00:50:33,977
demand.

606
00:50:34,268 --> 00:50:42,941
Two design decisions here matter enough to slow down and actually explain, and everything

607
00:50:43,232 --> 00:50:46,948
else can scroll past as configuration.

608
00:50:47,240 --> 00:50:49,717
The first is authentication.

609
00:50:50,009 --> 00:50:58,681
We use OpenID Connect through configure aws credentials, which means the workflow assumes an

610
00:50:58,972 --> 00:51:07,644
I A M role directly, and there are no long lived AWS access keys

611
00:51:07,935 --> 00:51:16,608
sitting in GitHub secrets, quietly waiting for someone to leak them in a public

612
00:51:16,899 --> 00:51:19,996
repo or a support ticket.

613
00:51:20,287 --> 00:51:28,959
This is worth calling out explicitly, because it's not just good practice, it's literally

614
00:51:29,251 --> 00:51:35,445
itself a piece of C C six point one evidence.

615
00:51:35,736 --> 00:51:40,072
The workflow doesn't just implement the control.

616
00:51:40,363 --> 00:51:43,461
The workflow is the control.

617
00:51:43,752 --> 00:51:46,230
The second is organization.

618
00:51:46,521 --> 00:51:54,574
Evidence lands in S3 organized strictly by date, not by workflow run number.

619
00:51:54,865 --> 00:52:03,537
That distinction matters enormously later, because when a Type Two auditor samples March fifteenth

620
00:52:03,828 --> 00:52:12,500
specifically, they get exactly one clean folder for that day, not a search through

621
00:52:12,792 --> 00:52:21,464
hundreds of historical workflow runs trying to figure out which one corresponds to which

622
00:52:21,755 --> 00:52:22,994
calendar date.

623
00:52:23,285 --> 00:52:30,718
Inside the workflow, we collect evidence control by control, every single night.

624
00:52:31,010 --> 00:52:39,682
For C C six point one, we capture our R B A C configuration

625
00:52:39,973 --> 00:52:46,787
directly from the cluster, roles and role bindings, in full YAML.

626
00:52:47,078 --> 00:52:55,750
For C C six point six, we query R D S for its encryption

627
00:52:56,041 --> 00:53:04,714
status and its K M S key rotation status, proving both that encryption is

628
00:53:05,005 --> 00:53:12,438
on and that the keys behind it are actually rotating on schedule.

629
00:53:12,729 --> 00:53:21,402
For C C six point seven, we run a live TLS handshake against our

630
00:53:21,693 --> 00:53:30,365
production endpoint, forcing TLS one point three specifically, and we capture the protocol, cipher,

631
00:53:30,656 --> 00:53:32,514
and verification result.

632
00:53:32,806 --> 00:53:41,478
For C C seven point one, we capture our horizontal pod autoscaler status and

633
00:53:41,769 --> 00:53:50,441
our pod disruption budget status, proving our availability controls are actually configured, not just

634
00:53:50,732 --> 00:53:51,971
documented somewhere.

635
00:53:52,262 --> 00:54:00,935
For C C seven point two, we capture our full set of Prometheus alerting

636
00:54:01,226 --> 00:54:02,126
rules.

637
00:54:02,417 --> 00:54:11,089
For C C seven point four, we capture our automated R D S snapshots

638
00:54:11,380 --> 00:54:14,478
from the last seven days.

639
00:54:14,769 --> 00:54:23,441
And for C C eight point one, we query GitHub's A P I directly

640
00:54:23,732 --> 00:54:32,404
for every merged pull request, capturing who authored it, who approved it, and exactly

641
00:54:32,696 --> 00:54:38,890
when it merged, a complete and tamper evident change history.

642
00:54:39,181 --> 00:54:47,854
Every night, all of that gets bundled into a manifest, a single JSON file

643
00:54:48,145 --> 00:54:56,817
recording the date, an exact timestamp, which workflow run produced it, which controls were

644
00:54:57,108 --> 00:55:00,205
covered, and an overall status.

645
00:55:00,496 --> 00:55:09,169
Then everything syncs to S3 with server side encryption using our own customer managed

646
00:55:09,460 --> 00:55:11,938
K M S key.

647
00:55:12,229 --> 00:55:20,901
And now, here's the part that turns this from a nice automation script into

648
00:55:21,192 --> 00:55:29,864
genuinely bulletproof evidence, and it's also the part with a real gotcha that nobody

649
00:55:30,156 --> 00:55:33,872
warns you about ahead of time.

650
00:55:34,163 --> 00:55:42,836
We provision this bucket with S3 Object Lock enabled, and Object Lock has to

651
00:55:43,127 --> 00:55:46,844
be turned on at bucket creation.

652
00:55:47,135 --> 00:55:55,807
You cannot add it later, full stop, so get this right the first time.

653
00:55:56,098 --> 00:56:04,770
We configure it in compliance mode, with a retention period of twenty five fifty

654
00:56:05,062 --> 00:56:07,539
five days, seven years.

655
00:56:07,830 --> 00:56:12,786
Compliance mode means exactly what it sounds like.

656
00:56:13,077 --> 00:56:21,749
Once an object is locked under this mode, no principal whatsoever, not the account

657
00:56:22,041 --> 00:56:30,713
root user, not even AWS Support acting on your behalf, can shorten that retention

658
00:56:31,004 --> 00:56:35,960
period or delete the object before it expires.

659
00:56:36,251 --> 00:56:44,923
That's genuinely the entire point, because it means your evidence is defensible even against

660
00:56:45,214 --> 00:56:53,886
a worst case insider threat scenario, someone with root access trying to cover their

661
00:56:54,178 --> 00:56:55,416
own tracks.

662
00:56:55,708 --> 00:57:04,380
But here's the gotcha, stated plainly, because this is exactly the kind of detail

663
00:57:04,671 --> 00:57:13,343
that only shows up after you've actually made this mistake once, or watched someone

664
00:57:13,634 --> 00:57:15,493
else make it.

665
00:57:15,784 --> 00:57:20,120
Compliance mode is a one way door.

666
00:57:20,411 --> 00:57:23,508
There is no undo button.

667
00:57:23,800 --> 00:57:32,472
A misconfigured seven year retention period on the wrong bucket is a mistake you

668
00:57:32,763 --> 00:57:38,338
cannot fix, only wait out, for seven full years.

669
00:57:38,629 --> 00:57:47,301
So test this thoroughly, end to end, in a completely disposable throwaway bucket first.

670
00:57:47,593 --> 00:57:55,645
Never validate compliance mode directly against the bucket you actually intend to keep.

671
00:57:55,937 --> 00:58:04,609
AWS gives you governance mode specifically as the reversible alternative, use that everywhere in

672
00:58:04,900 --> 00:58:13,572
staging, and only flip to compliance mode in production once you've validated the entire

673
00:58:13,863 --> 00:58:16,961
pipeline works exactly as intended.

674
00:58:17,252 --> 00:58:21,588
Let's verify the whole thing actually works.

675
00:58:21,879 --> 00:58:24,357
Trigger the workflow manually.

676
00:58:24,648 --> 00:58:33,320
Watch evidence land in S3, organized cleanly by today's date, one folder per control.

677
00:58:33,611 --> 00:58:42,284
And then run the actual immutability test that proves this isn't just theoretical protection.

678
00:58:42,575 --> 00:58:48,150
Try to delete a file from that locked bucket.

679
00:58:48,441 --> 00:58:56,494
It should fail, loudly, with access denied, object is locked in compliance mode.

680
00:58:56,785 --> 00:59:00,502
That failure is the entire point.

681
00:59:00,793 --> 00:59:09,465
That's your immutable audit trail working exactly as designed, and it's the single strongest

682
00:59:09,756 --> 00:59:18,428
piece of infrastructure you'll build in this entire phase, because from this point forward,

683
00:59:18,720 --> 00:59:26,772
evidence collection is no longer something your team has to remember to do.

684
00:59:27,064 --> 00:59:32,639
It happens whether anyone thinks about it or not.

685
00:59:32,930 --> 00:59:37,266
Commit the workflow and the Terraform together.

686
00:59:37,557 --> 00:59:46,229
Next lecture, we tackle a decision that costs real companies real deals when they

687
00:59:46,520 --> 00:59:48,379
get it wrong.

688
00:59:48,670 --> 00:59:56,103
Type One or Type Two, and exactly when to choose each one.

689
00:59:56,394 --> 01:00:05,067
Let's talk about a failure mode in evidence automation that's genuinely common and genuinely

690
01:00:05,358 --> 01:00:14,030
avoidable, which is treating the nightly workflow itself as if it were infallible simply

691
01:00:14,321 --> 01:00:16,180
because it's automated.

692
01:00:16,471 --> 01:00:25,143
Automation removes the human forgetting to do something, but it introduces a new risk,

693
01:00:25,434 --> 01:00:34,106
silent failure, where the workflow runs, appears green in your GitHub Actions history, and

694
01:00:34,398 --> 01:00:43,070
quietly produces an empty or malformed evidence file because some upstream command failed in

695
01:00:43,361 --> 01:00:48,316
a way the workflow didn't explicitly check for.

696
01:00:48,608 --> 01:00:57,280
This is exactly why our evidence assembly script, which we'll build properly in a

697
01:00:57,571 --> 01:01:06,243
later lecture, includes real validation logic rather than trusting that a workflow marked successful

698
01:01:06,534 --> 01:01:09,012
actually produced meaningful evidence.

699
01:01:09,303 --> 01:01:13,020
Automated does not mean unattended forever.

700
01:01:13,311 --> 01:01:21,983
It means the human attention moves from doing the collection to monitoring whether the

701
01:01:22,275 --> 01:01:24,133
collection actually worked.

702
01:01:24,424 --> 01:01:33,096
Let's also talk about retention strategy beyond just the seven year Object Lock period,

703
01:01:33,388 --> 01:01:42,060
because seven years of daily evidence, uncompressed, across every control, adds up to real

704
01:01:42,351 --> 01:01:51,023
storage cost over time, and a genuinely senior implementation thinks about lifecycle policy from

705
01:01:51,314 --> 01:01:58,128
day one rather than discovering the cost problem two years in.

706
01:01:58,419 --> 01:02:07,092
A sensible pattern here is tiering by age, keeping the most recent observation period's

707
01:02:07,383 --> 01:02:16,055
evidence in standard S3 storage for fast auditor access, then transitioning older evidence, past

708
01:02:16,346 --> 01:02:25,018
your current audit cycle, into Glacier or Glacier Deep Archive, where retrieval is slower

709
01:02:25,310 --> 01:02:33,982
but storage cost drops enormously, while the Object Lock retention clock keeps running regardless

710
01:02:34,273 --> 01:02:36,131
of storage class.

711
01:02:36,423 --> 01:02:45,095
You're not deleting anything early, compliance mode wouldn't let you anyway, you're just being

712
01:02:45,386 --> 01:02:48,483
deliberate about where it lives.

713
01:02:48,774 --> 01:02:57,447
And here's a smaller but genuinely important detail about the Slack alerting step in

714
01:02:57,738 --> 01:02:58,977
our workflow.

715
01:02:59,268 --> 01:03:07,940
An alert that fires on failure is only useful if somebody is actually watching

716
01:03:08,231 --> 01:03:13,806
that channel and empowered to act on it immediately.

717
01:03:14,097 --> 01:03:22,770
Route this specifically to your on call rotation, not to a general engineering channel

718
01:03:23,061 --> 01:03:31,733
where it can sit unread for two days, because a failed evidence collection run

719
01:03:32,024 --> 01:03:40,696
on a day that later gets sampled by an auditor is exactly the kind

720
01:03:40,988 --> 01:03:49,660
of gap that turns into a real finding, and the difference between catching that

721
01:03:49,951 --> 01:03:58,623
failure the same night versus discovering it months later during fieldwork is entirely a

722
01:03:58,914 --> 01:04:05,109
function of who actually saw the alert and how quickly.

723
01:04:05,400 --> 01:04:14,072
Let's go one level deeper on the actual IAM role you assume through OIDC,

724
01:04:14,363 --> 01:04:23,036
because the scope of permissions on that role matters just as much as the

725
01:04:23,327 --> 01:04:25,185
authentication mechanism itself.

726
01:04:25,476 --> 01:04:34,149
A genuinely well built evidence collector role has read only permissions, full stop, scoped

727
01:04:34,440 --> 01:04:43,112
precisely to the specific describe and get style API calls each collection step actually

728
01:04:43,403 --> 01:04:45,261
needs, nothing broader.

