First.

934
01:25:33,846 --> 01:25:38,987
How do you ensure only authorized users access the system.

935
01:25:39,266 --> 01:25:45,950
The answer is defense in depth, layered, with no single point of failure.

936
01:25:46,229 --> 01:25:53,427
Every request requires a valid A P I key, hashed with SHA two fifty

937
01:25:53,706 --> 01:26:00,904
six, never stored in plaintext, carrying a ninety day time to live, and revocable

938
01:26:01,184 --> 01:26:03,754
immediately with no grace period.

939
01:26:04,034 --> 01:26:11,231
Internally, Vault issues unique database credentials per pod, each with a one hour time

940
01:26:11,511 --> 01:26:12,539
to live.

941
01:26:12,819 --> 01:26:20,016
Underneath that, Kubernetes R B A C and Cilium's zero trust network policies enforce

942
01:26:20,296 --> 01:26:24,923
exactly what each service account is allowed to touch.

943
01:26:25,202 --> 01:26:32,400
And then you open the actual middleware file, live, on screen, and show them

944
01:26:32,680 --> 01:26:36,792
the code doing exactly what you just described.

945
01:26:37,072 --> 01:26:37,972
Second.

946
01:26:38,251 --> 01:26:42,878
How do you detect and respond to security incidents.

947
01:26:43,158 --> 01:26:50,356
Falco runs on every single node through a DaemonSet, using e B P F

948
01:26:50,635 --> 01:26:57,833
probes to monitor system calls in real time, with custom rules written specifically for

949
01:26:58,112 --> 01:27:05,310
our workload, alerting the moment a pod attempts an unexpected outbound connection or an

950
01:27:05,589 --> 01:27:07,132
unexpected shell execution.

951
01:27:07,411 --> 01:27:12,553
Alerts route through Falcosidekick to PagerDuty with severity based escalation.

952
01:27:12,832 --> 01:27:20,030
Our incident response runbook lives in Git, version controlled, reviewed quarterly, and every single

953
01:27:20,309 --> 01:27:27,507
security event generates a formal post incident review that itself becomes Type Two evidence.

954
01:27:27,786 --> 01:27:28,686
Third.

955
01:27:28,966 --> 01:27:34,107
How do you ensure data integrity and prevent unauthorized changes.

956
01:27:34,386 --> 01:27:41,584
Every SEC filing gets SHA two fifty six hashed before storage, so ingesting the

957
01:27:41,864 --> 01:27:48,033
same filing twice simply skips the duplicate, making the entire pipeline idempotent.

958
01:27:48,313 --> 01:27:55,510
Our analysis history table is append only by design, with PostgreSQL triggers physically blocking

959
01:27:55,790 --> 01:28:02,987
update and delete statements, plus hash chaining across records so any tampering becomes immediately,

960
01:28:03,267 --> 01:28:04,295
mathematically detectable.

961
01:28:04,575 --> 01:28:11,772
Every infrastructure change goes through a pull request requiring two reviewers, with CODEOWNERS routing

962
01:28:12,052 --> 01:28:16,165
security touching files specifically to the security team.

963
01:28:16,444 --> 01:28:23,642
And ArgoCD manages all cluster state from Git, so if anyone ever runs a

964
01:28:23,921 --> 01:28:31,119
manual kubectl change directly against the cluster, drift detection catches it within seconds and

965
01:28:31,399 --> 01:28:37,054
self heals it back to what Git says should be true.

966
01:28:37,333 --> 01:28:38,233
Fourth.

967
01:28:38,513 --> 01:28:43,654
How do you ensure the system meets its availability commitments.

968
01:28:43,933 --> 01:28:51,131
We target ninety nine point nine percent availability, which is forty three minutes of

969
01:28:51,411 --> 01:28:58,608
allowed downtime per month, and the architecture has genuinely no single point of failure

970
01:28:58,888 --> 01:28:59,788
anywhere.

971
01:29:00,067 --> 01:29:06,751
R D S runs multi A Z with automatic failover under two minutes.

972
01:29:07,030 --> 01:29:12,686
E K S spans three availability zones with topology spread constraints.

973
01:29:12,965 --> 01:29:20,163
Horizontal pod autoscaling takes us from three replicas to twenty based on real CPU

974
01:29:20,442 --> 01:29:21,342
utilization.

975
01:29:21,622 --> 01:29:28,819
Karpenter provisions brand new nodes in under thirty seconds during a scale up event.

976
01:29:29,099 --> 01:29:36,297
And pod disruption budgets guarantee a minimum of two A P I replicas running

977
01:29:36,576 --> 01:29:40,689
at all times, even during active node maintenance.

978
01:29:40,969 --> 01:29:41,869
Fifth.

979
01:29:42,148 --> 01:29:46,261
How do you manage third party vendor risk.

980
01:29:46,540 --> 01:29:53,738
We maintain a tiered vendor register, critical, high, medium, low, with reassessment frequency scaled

981
01:29:54,018 --> 01:29:56,074
directly to that tier.

982
01:29:56,354 --> 01:30:03,551
Critical vendors, AWS and OpenAI, get reassessed quarterly, with their own SOC two reports

983
01:30:03,831 --> 01:30:07,944
reviewed and executed Data Processing Addendums on file.

984
01:30:08,223 --> 01:30:15,421
AWS specifically runs through the Shared Responsibility Model, documented explicitly so everyone knows exactly

985
01:30:15,700 --> 01:30:19,299
what AWS owns versus what we own.

986
01:30:19,579 --> 01:30:26,776
And OpenAI is configured with a zero retention policy, since no personally identifiable information

987
01:30:27,056 --> 01:30:32,197
is ever sent in a query payload to begin with.

988
01:30:32,476 --> 01:30:39,160
And here's the one rule that matters more than any individual answer above.

989
01:30:39,440 --> 01:30:45,609
If you genuinely don't know something in the room, say so, plainly.

990
01:30:45,888 --> 01:30:49,487
Let me verify that and follow up.

991
01:30:49,767 --> 01:30:51,309
Do not guess.

992
01:30:51,589 --> 01:30:58,786
Auditors treat a wrong answer discovered later as significantly worse than an honestly admitted

993
01:30:59,066 --> 01:31:05,749
gap in the moment, and there's a real reason for that beyond etiquette.

994
01:31:06,029 --> 01:31:13,227
You're operating under a signed management assertion, and fabrication under that assertion carries consequences

995
01:31:13,506 --> 01:31:16,591
well beyond just a routine finding.

996
01:31:16,870 --> 01:31:19,955
This isn't a soft skills tip.

997
01:31:20,234 --> 01:31:27,432
It's the mathematically correct response to the actual incentive structure of the entire engagement.

998
01:31:27,712 --> 01:31:34,909
Practice these five questions with whoever will genuinely be in that room, not by

999
01:31:35,189 --> 01:31:42,386
memorizing a script, but by running the live evidence command until pulling it up

1000
01:31:42,666 --> 01:31:44,208
is completely automatic.

1001
01:31:44,488 --> 01:31:51,685
Next lecture, we build the tracker that turns every gap we've surfaced across this

1002
01:31:51,965 --> 01:31:56,592
entire phase into an owned, dated, evidenced remediation plan.

1003
01:31:56,871 --> 01:32:04,069
Let's go deeper on how sample size actually scales, because the mental model matters

1004
01:32:04,349 --> 01:32:08,976
more than the exact formula an auditor uses internally.

1005
01:32:09,255 --> 01:32:13,882
Broadly, auditors think in terms of population and frequency.

1006
01:32:14,162 --> 01:32:21,359
A control tested daily across a full year gives you a population of roughly

1007
01:32:21,639 --> 01:32:28,837
two hundred fifty business days, and a typical sample from a population that size

1008
01:32:29,116 --> 01:32:36,314
might land somewhere in the twenty five to forty range, enough to give the

1009
01:32:36,593 --> 01:32:43,791
auditor statistical confidence the control operated consistently without needing to review every single day.

1010
01:32:44,070 --> 01:32:51,268
A control tested quarterly gives you a population of four, and at that population

1011
01:32:51,548 --> 01:32:58,745
size, sampling doesn't really apply in any meaningful sense, the auditor typically just reviews

1012
01:32:59,025 --> 01:33:00,053
all four.

1013
01:33:00,332 --> 01:33:07,530
This is exactly why less frequent controls, quarterly restore tests being the clearest example,

1014
01:33:07,810 --> 01:33:15,007
actually carry more individual weight per instance than a daily control does, because there's

1015
01:33:15,287 --> 01:33:20,942
no averaging out a single bad instance across a larger sample.

1016
01:33:21,222 --> 01:33:28,419
Here's a genuinely practical implication of that math for how you should prepare your

1017
01:33:28,699 --> 01:33:31,269
team before the actual walkthrough.

1018
01:33:31,549 --> 01:33:35,662
Don't just rehearse your happy path evidence commands.

1019
01:33:35,941 --> 01:33:43,139
Rehearse what you'd say if the auditor's sample happens to land on the one

1020
01:33:43,418 --> 01:33:50,616
day where something looked slightly unusual, a deployment that took longer than normal, an

1021
01:33:50,896 --> 01:33:57,579
alert that fired and required actual investigation, a backup that needed a retry.

1022
01:33:57,859 --> 01:34:00,943
These moments are not automatically findings.

1023
01:34:01,223 --> 01:34:08,421
An auditor who sees your team detect a real event, respond according to the

1024
01:34:08,700 --> 01:34:15,898
documented runbook, and generate a clean post incident review, is actually seeing your controls

1025
01:34:16,177 --> 01:34:18,234
work exactly as designed.

1026
01:34:18,513 --> 01:34:25,711
The failure mode isn't having an incident during your observation period, every real system

1027
01:34:25,990 --> 01:34:26,890
does.

1028
01:34:27,170 --> 01:34:34,367
The failure mode is not being able to show, clearly and quickly, that your

1029
01:34:34,647 --> 01:34:38,246
response process actually functioned when it mattered.

1030
01:34:38,525 --> 01:34:45,723
One last thing worth internalizing about the entrance conference specifically, beyond the five technical

1031
01:34:46,002 --> 01:34:46,902
questions.

1032
01:34:47,182 --> 01:34:54,380
Auditors are also quietly assessing whether your team communicates consistently with each other, not

1033
01:34:54,659 --> 01:34:56,201
just with them.

1034
01:34:56,481 --> 01:35:03,679
If your security lead describes a control one way and your platform lead describes

1035
01:35:03,958 --> 01:35:11,156
the same control slightly differently in the same room, that inconsistency itself becomes a

1036
01:35:11,435 --> 01:35:18,633
data point, suggesting the control might not be as well understood internally as your

1037
01:35:18,912 --> 01:35:19,941
documentation claims.

1038
01:35:20,220 --> 01:35:27,418
Before the actual walkthrough, get your team in a room together and have each

1039
01:35:27,697 --> 01:35:34,895
person explain, out loud, the control they personally own, to each other, not just

1040
01:35:35,174 --> 01:35:37,231
to an imagined auditor.

1041
01:35:37,510 --> 01:35:44,708
The goal isn't a rehearsed script, it's genuine shared understanding, and the difference between

1042
01:35:44,988 --> 01:35:52,185
those two things is exactly what a good auditor is trained to notice within

1043
01:35:52,465 --> 01:35:55,549
the first ten minutes of conversation.

1044
01:35:55,829 --> 01:36:03,027
Let's talk about a specific trap that catches even well prepared teams during the

1045
01:36:03,306 --> 01:36:04,334
walkthrough, over-explaining.

1046
01:36:04,614 --> 01:36:11,811
When an auditor asks a direct question, the correct instinct is a direct, complete,

1047
01:36:12,091 --> 01:36:19,289
but bounded answer, followed by silence, not a continuation into three adjacent topics nobody

1048
01:36:19,568 --> 01:36:20,596
asked about.

1049
01:36:20,876 --> 01:36:28,074
Every extra thing you volunteer is a new thread the auditor might reasonably pull

1050
01:36:28,353 --> 01:36:35,551
on, and a thread you volunteered but weren't actually prepared to defend in depth

1051
01:36:35,830 --> 01:36:39,943
creates unnecessary risk you didn't need to introduce.

1052
01:36:40,223 --> 01:36:47,420
Answer exactly what was asked, thoroughly and honestly, show the live evidence, and then

1053
01:36:47,700 --> 01:36:48,728
stop talking.

1054
01:36:49,007 --> 01:36:56,205
If they want more, they'll ask a specific follow up, and you answer that

1055
01:36:56,485 --> 01:36:58,541
one just as precisely.

1056
01:36:58,821 --> 01:37:04,990
Here's a final practical tip worth building into your actual walkthrough logistics.

1057
01:37:05,269 --> 01:37:12,467
Have every single live evidence command you might need pre-typed and ready in a

1058
01:37:12,747 --> 01:37:19,944
terminal, in a dedicated tab or window, before the call even starts, not something

1059
01:37:20,224 --> 01:37:26,393
you're constructing live under mild pressure while an auditor watches you type.

1060
01:37:26,673 --> 01:37:33,870
The content of your answer matters enormously, but so does the visible fluency of

1061
01:37:34,150 --> 01:37:41,348
producing the proof, because a team that pulls up exactly the right command in

1062
01:37:41,627 --> 01:37:48,825
three seconds looks, correctly, like a team that runs this evidence pipeline constantly as

1063
01:37:49,104 --> 01:37:56,302
part of normal operations, not one that's assembling a demonstration specifically for this call.

1064
01:37:58,302 --> 01:38:01,802
LECTURE 7 — REMEDIATION PLANNING

1065
01:38:02,802 --> 01:38:10,418
A gap you know about and haven't fixed yet is not a neutral, harmless

1066
01:38:10,706 --> 01:38:13,969
thing sitting quietly on a backlog.

1067
01:38:14,257 --> 01:38:21,873
During your Type Two observation period specifically, it is active evidence that the control

1068
01:38:22,161 --> 01:38:29,777
was genuinely absent for however long it took your team to notice and act.

1069
01:38:30,064 --> 01:38:36,048
That reframing is the entire reason a real remediation tracker exists.

1070
01:38:36,336 --> 01:38:43,952
It converts we'll get to it eventually into a named owner, a specific due

1071
01:38:44,240 --> 01:38:51,855
date, and, if the timeline ever slips, a defensible reason why, written down before

1072
01:38:52,143 --> 01:38:53,775
an auditor asks.

1073
01:38:54,063 --> 01:39:01,679
Let's build this properly, with severity that actually drives urgency, not severity as decoration.

1074
01:39:01,966 --> 01:39:09,582
Critical gaps block your Type One entirely, full stop, and must close before fieldwork

1075
01:39:09,870 --> 01:39:10,958
even begins.

1076
01:39:11,246 --> 01:39:18,861
High gaps survive a Type One just fine, but they become genuine Type Two

1077
01:39:19,149 --> 01:39:24,589
findings if they're still open when your observation period starts.

1078
01:39:24,877 --> 01:39:32,493
Medium gaps are acceptable, but only with a documented compensating control explaining exactly why

1079
01:39:32,780 --> 01:39:36,588
the risk is managed despite the gap.

1080
01:39:36,876 --> 01:39:44,492
And low gaps are legitimate roadmap items that almost never generate an actual finding

1081
01:39:44,780 --> 01:39:46,412
on their own.

1082
01:39:46,699 --> 01:39:54,315
Let's walk through four real gaps, exactly the kind you'll actually encounter, each with

1083
01:39:54,603 --> 01:40:01,131
the specific remediation steps that make the fix concrete instead of vague.

1084
01:40:01,419 --> 01:40:03,051
Gap one, critical.

1085
01:40:03,338 --> 01:40:10,954
No A P I key rotation policy enforced, mapping to control C C six

1086
01:40:11,242 --> 01:40:12,330
point one.

1087
01:40:12,618 --> 01:40:18,057
Think about what a key that never expires actually is.

1088
01:40:18,345 --> 01:40:25,961
It's a password that never changes, and if it ever leaks, it stays compromised

1089
01:40:26,249 --> 01:40:29,513
forever, silently, with nobody the wiser.

1090
01:40:29,801 --> 01:40:37,416
The fix, a rotation Lambda triggered by EventBridge on a fourteen day notice window,

1091
01:40:37,704 --> 01:40:45,320
a new notification tracking column on the A P I keys table, a staging

1092
01:40:45,608 --> 01:40:53,223
test that verifies an old key genuinely gets rejected after rotation completes, and the

1093
01:40:53,511 --> 01:40:58,407
whole policy documented in a dedicated security policy file.

1094
01:40:58,695 --> 01:41:06,311
Evidence required, the rotation Lambda's A R N plus the very first rotation log

1095
01:41:06,598 --> 01:41:07,686
it produces.

1096
01:41:07,974 --> 01:41:15,590
Gap two, also critical, and this one is subtle enough that teams miss it

1097
01:41:15,878 --> 01:41:16,778
constantly.

1098
01:41:17,066 --> 01:41:20,873
R D S staging environment not encrypted.

1099
01:41:21,161 --> 01:41:28,777
Most teams encrypt production diligently and then think, it's just staging, it doesn't really

1100
01:41:29,065 --> 01:41:29,965
matter.

1101
01:41:30,253 --> 01:41:33,517
Here's exactly why that reasoning fails.

1102
01:41:33,804 --> 01:41:41,420
Staging routinely contains production like data, and auditors check every single environment that falls

1103
01:41:41,708 --> 01:41:47,692
within scope, not just the one you assumed they'd care about.

1104
01:41:47,979 --> 01:41:53,963
If staging is in scope, it must be encrypted, no exceptions.

1105
01:41:54,251 --> 01:42:01,867
The fix, create an encrypted snapshot of the existing staging database, restore it as

1106
01:42:02,155 --> 01:42:09,770
a new encrypted instance, cut the application connection string over, validate for forty eight

1107
01:42:10,058 --> 01:42:17,674
hours, then delete the original unencrypted instance, and update your Terraform so storage encryption

1108
01:42:17,962 --> 01:42:24,490
defaults to true across every single environment going forward, not just production.

1109
01:42:24,777 --> 01:42:26,953
Gap three, high severity.

1110
01:42:27,241 --> 01:42:33,769
No vendor assessment for Groq, despite it running in production since March.

1111
01:42:34,057 --> 01:42:37,865
This is a vendor risk gap, plainly.

1112
01:42:38,152 --> 01:42:45,768
Groq is live, handling real traffic, and nobody ever formally assessed their security posture.

1113
01:42:46,056 --> 01:42:53,672
The fix, request their SOC two report or security questionnaire directly, complete our own

1114
01:42:53,959 --> 01:43:01,575
internal vendor questionnaire, execute a Data Processing Addendum, and add the entry to our

1115
01:43:01,863 --> 01:43:06,215
vendor register with a real assessment date attached.

1116
01:43:06,503 --> 01:43:09,223
Gap four, also high severity.

1117
01:43:09,510 --> 01:43:14,406
No quarterly restore test has ever actually been performed.

1118
01:43:14,694 --> 01:43:21,766
Backups that have never been tested are, functionally, a belief, not a control.

1119
01:43:22,054 --> 01:43:29,669
Having a backup is like having a lifeboat on a ship, it's only genuinely

1120
01:43:29,957 --> 01:43:37,573
useful if someone has actually confirmed how to use it and that it actually

1121
01:43:37,861 --> 01:43:38,761
floats.

1122
01:43:39,049 --> 01:43:46,664
The fix, write an actual restore test script, run the very first restore test

1123
01:43:46,952 --> 01:43:54,568
against staging, document the results including a measured recovery time objective, and schedule it

1124
01:43:54,856 --> 01:43:58,664
to run automatically every quarter going forward.

1125
01:43:58,951 --> 01:44:06,567
Now, here's a mechanism most explanations of remediation planning skip entirely, and it's genuinely

1126
01:44:06,855 --> 01:44:10,119
reassuring once you understand it correctly.

1127
01:44:10,407 --> 01:44:18,022
If a gap isn't fully closed by the time fieldwork happens, that doesn't automatically

1128
01:44:18,310 --> 01:44:19,942
sink your report.

1129
01:44:20,230 --> 01:44:27,846
It typically becomes a qualified opinion, or a noted exception, and critically, you get

1130
01:44:28,133 --> 01:44:35,749
to attach a management response, a written statement explaining what happened, why, and your

1131
01:44:36,037 --> 01:44:38,757
specific remediation timeline going forward.

1132
01:44:39,045 --> 01:44:46,661
Auditors, and sophisticated customers reading the report, both genuinely understand that no system is

1133
01:44:46,948 --> 01:44:48,580
ever completely gapless.

1134
01:44:48,868 --> 01:44:56,484
What they're actually assessing is whether you know your own gaps and are managing

1135
01:44:56,772 --> 01:44:58,948
them honestly and visibly.

1136
01:44:59,235 --> 01:45:06,851
A tracker with dated, owned, evidenced remediation is exactly what makes that management response

1137
01:45:07,139 --> 01:45:10,403
land as credible instead of defensive.

1138
01:45:10,691 --> 01:45:18,306
Build a weekly cadence around this tracker, because a tracker nobody looks at again

1139
01:45:18,594 --> 01:45:22,402
is worse than useless, it's false confidence.

1140
01:45:22,690 --> 01:45:30,306
We wrote a small script that checks every remediation item's due date against today,

1141
01:45:30,593 --> 01:45:38,209
and fails loudly, with a nonzero exit code, whenever something is overdue and still

1142
01:45:38,497 --> 01:45:40,673
marked as not done.

1143
01:45:40,961 --> 01:45:42,593
Run it weekly.

1144
01:45:42,880 --> 01:45:50,496
It keeps the whole team honest, and it prevents exactly the kind of last

1145
01:45:50,784 --> 01:45:58,400
minute panic that happens when someone realizes, three days before fieldwork, that a critical

1146
01:45:58,688 --> 01:46:01,951
item quietly slipped for two months.

1147
01:46:02,239 --> 01:46:07,679
The actual skill here was never the tracker's file format.

1148
01:46:07,967 --> 01:46:10,143
It's the prioritization discipline.

1149
01:46:10,431 --> 01:46:14,239
Critical gaps close before fieldwork, full stop.

1150
01:46:14,526 --> 01:46:19,966
High gaps close before your Type Two observation window opens.

1151
01:46:20,254 --> 01:46:25,694
Medium gaps get a documented compensating control instead of silence.

1152
01:46:25,982 --> 01:46:30,877
Low gaps go on the roadmap where they belong.

1153
01:46:31,165 --> 01:46:37,693
Get that prioritization right, and the tracker basically runs itself from there.

1154
01:46:37,981 --> 01:46:45,597
Let's talk about how to actually run the weekly remediation review meeting itself, because

1155
01:46:45,884 --> 01:46:53,500
the tracker file is only as good as the discipline around looking at it.

1156
01:46:53,788 --> 01:47:01,404
Keep this meeting genuinely short, fifteen minutes is enough, and structure it around exactly

1157
01:47:01,691 --> 01:47:04,955
three questions for every open item.

1158
01:47:05,243 --> 01:47:07,963
What changed since last week.

1159
01:47:08,251 --> 01:47:13,147
Is the due date still realistic given what changed.

1160
01:47:13,435 --> 01:47:21,050
And does anything here need to be escalated above the owner's pay grade because

1161
01:47:21,338 --> 01:47:26,778
it's now blocked on a decision only leadership can make.

1162
01:47:27,066 --> 01:47:34,681
Teams that let this meeting sprawl into a general engineering standup lose the specific

1163
01:47:34,969 --> 01:47:42,585
focus that makes a remediation tracker actually work, which is ruthless, weekly accountability against

1164
01:47:42,873 --> 01:47:46,681
dated commitments, nothing more and nothing less.

1165
01:47:46,969 --> 01:47:54,584
Here's a nuance worth adding to how you think about medium severity, accepted risk

1166
01:47:54,872 --> 01:48:02,488
items specifically, because accepted risk isn't the same thing as ignored risk, and auditors

1167
01:48:02,776 --> 01:48:05,496
can absolutely tell the difference.

1168
01:48:05,783 --> 01:48:13,399
A properly documented accepted risk includes not just what the gap is, but a

1169
01:48:13,687 --> 01:48:21,303
specific compensating control that's already active today, a specific reason the residual risk is

1170
01:48:21,590 --> 01:48:29,206
tolerable given that compensating control, and a specific review date where someone will reassess

1171
01:48:29,494 --> 01:48:32,758
whether the acceptance still makes sense.

1172
01:48:33,046 --> 01:48:40,661
An accepted risk with no review date attached tends to quietly become permanent, and

1173
01:48:40,949 --> 01:48:48,565
a permanent accepted risk that's never actually been revisited starts to look, to an

1174
01:48:48,853 --> 01:48:56,469
experienced auditor, exactly like a gap somebody simply decided not to fix rather than

1175
01:48:56,756 --> 01:48:58,932
a genuinely managed risk.

1176
01:48:59,220 --> 01:49:06,836
And finally, let's talk about the moment right before fieldwork begins, when your critical

1177
01:49:07,124 --> 01:49:14,739
and high severity items should already be closed, and what to do if one

1178
01:49:15,027 --> 01:49:19,379
is stubbornly still open despite your best planning.

1179
01:49:19,667 --> 01:49:27,283
The honest move here is not to hide it and hope the sample doesn't

1180
01:49:27,570 --> 01:49:29,202
land on it.

1181
01:49:29,490 --> 01:49:37,106
The honest move is to raise it proactively with your auditor before fieldwork starts,

1182
01:49:37,394 --> 01:49:45,010
explain exactly where you are, and ask what they'd need to see to still

1183
01:49:45,297 --> 01:49:51,281
consider the broader control environment sound despite this one open item.

1184
01:49:51,569 --> 01:49:59,185
Auditors deal with this situation constantly, it is not unusual to them, and firms

1185
01:49:59,473 --> 01:50:07,088
that see a team surface their own remaining gap ahead of time, with a

1186
01:50:07,376 --> 01:50:14,992
credible plan already attached, generally treat that transparency as a positive signal about the

1187
01:50:15,280 --> 01:50:20,719
overall program, not as an automatic black mark against it.

1188
01:50:21,007 --> 01:50:28,623
Let's talk about a category of remediation item that's easy to underestimate specifically because

1189
01:50:28,911 --> 01:50:35,439
it doesn't show up as a technical gap at all, documentation gaps.

1190
01:50:35,726 --> 01:50:43,342
A control that's genuinely well implemented in code but has no corresponding written policy

1191
01:50:43,630 --> 01:50:51,246
describing it is still a finding waiting to happen, because auditors test both the

1192
01:50:51,534 --> 01:50:57,517
technical implementation and the existence of a documented policy governing it.

1193
01:50:57,805 --> 01:51:05,421
If your incident response process lives entirely in the collective memory of two senior

1194
01:51:05,709 --> 01:51:13,324
engineers and nowhere in writing, that's a remediation item, severity high, regardless of how

1195
01:51:13,612 --> 01:51:20,140
good those two engineers actually are at handling incidents when they happen.

1196
01:51:20,428 --> 01:51:28,044
Write it down, in Git, version controlled, reviewed on a real cadence, exactly like

1197
01:51:28,331 --> 01:51:32,139
every other control artifact in this phase.

1198
01:51:32,427 --> 01:51:40,043
And let's talk about the emotional dimension of remediation planning honestly, because it's rarely

1199
01:51:40,331 --> 01:51:44,138
addressed directly and it genuinely affects execution.

1200
01:51:44,426 --> 01:51:52,042
Nobody enjoys maintaining a list of their own system's shortcomings in a shared, visible

1201
01:51:52,330 --> 01:51:53,230
document.

1202
01:51:53,518 --> 01:52:01,133
The instinct to under-report, to mark something in progress when it's genuinely not started,

1203
01:52:01,421 --> 01:52:09,037
or to quietly narrow a gap's description until it sounds smaller than it actually

1204
01:52:09,325 --> 01:52:15,309
is, is completely human and completely corrosive to the entire exercise.

1205
01:52:15,596 --> 01:52:23,212
The teams that get real value out of a remediation tracker are the ones

1206
01:52:23,500 --> 01:52:31,116
that have explicitly, culturally, decoupled finding a gap from any sense of individual blame.

1207
01:52:31,404 --> 01:52:39,019
A gap discovered internally, before an auditor ever sees it, is a genuine win

1208
01:52:39,307 --> 01:52:46,923
for the team that found it, not a mark against them, and building that

1209
01:52:47,211 --> 01:52:54,826
culture deliberately is worth more than any specific YAML structure we've covered in this

1210
01:52:55,114 --> 01:52:56,014
lecture.

1211
01:52:58,014 --> 01:53:01,514
LECTURE 8 — EVIDENCE ASSEMBLY AND PHASE SYNTHESIS

1212
01:53:02,514 --> 01:53:11,727
Everything in this entire phase converges right here, in one single command that produces

1213
01:53:12,016 --> 01:53:17,938
a complete, timestamped, sampling ready evidence package on demand.

1214
01:53:18,228 --> 01:53:27,440
And here's why this specific moment matters more than it might seem to on

1215
01:53:27,730 --> 01:53:29,046
the surface.

1216
01:53:29,335 --> 01:53:38,548
An auditor's confidence in your entire program gets set by how you respond to

1217
01:53:38,837 --> 01:53:42,127
their very first evidence request.

1218
01:53:42,416 --> 01:53:51,629
A five minute turnaround versus a scramble through CloudWatch logs changes the entire tone

1219
01:53:51,918 --> 01:54:01,131
of everything that follows in the engagement, not because auditors are being petty about

1220
01:54:01,420 --> 01:54:10,633
it, but because a fast, clean response is itself, genuinely, evidence of a functioning

1221
01:54:10,922 --> 01:54:13,554
control environment underneath it.

1222
01:54:13,843 --> 01:54:21,740
The package structure mirrors exactly what we've built across this whole phase.

1223
01:54:22,029 --> 01:54:25,977
One dated folder per evidence date.

1224
01:54:26,267 --> 01:54:35,479
Inside it, a manifest, and then one subdirectory per control, each holding its actual

1225
01:54:35,769 --> 01:54:37,085
evidence files.

1226
01:54:37,374 --> 01:54:46,587
Alongside that, our policies and our runbooks, folded directly into the package, because a

1227
01:54:46,876 --> 01:54:54,772
security program is documentation plus proof together, never one without the other.

1228
01:54:55,062 --> 01:55:04,274
The assembly script itself takes an optional date argument, defaulting to today if you

1229
01:55:04,564 --> 01:55:13,776
don't specify one, which matters because you'll absolutely need to regenerate a package for

1230
01:55:14,065 --> 01:55:21,962
some specific date months from now when an auditor's sample lands there.

1231
01:55:22,251 --> 01:55:28,174
It creates every control directory in a single command.

1232
01:55:28,463 --> 01:55:37,676
It runs through C C six point one, pulling live R B A C

1233
01:55:37,965 --> 01:55:38,865
configuration.

1234
01:55:39,154 --> 01:55:48,367
C C six point six, pulling R D S encryption status and K M

1235
01:55:48,656 --> 01:55:51,288
S rotation status together.

1236
01:55:51,577 --> 01:55:58,816
C C six point seven, running a live TLS handshake test.

1237
01:55:59,105 --> 01:56:08,318
C C seven point one, pulling H P A status, pod disruption budget status,

1238
01:56:08,607 --> 01:56:13,871
and R D S multi A Z status.

1239
01:56:14,161 --> 01:56:20,741
C C seven point two, pulling every Prometheus alerting rule.

1240
01:56:21,030 --> 01:56:30,243
C C seven point four, pulling Velero backups and R D S snapshots side

1241
01:56:30,532 --> 01:56:31,848
by side.

1242
01:56:32,138 --> 01:56:41,350
And C C eight point one, pulling branch protection settings and full deployment history

1243
01:56:41,639 --> 01:56:42,539
together.

1244
01:56:42,829 --> 01:56:52,041
Two design decisions here genuinely matter more than the rest of the script combined,

1245
01:56:52,331 --> 01:56:56,937
so let's slow down specifically for these.

1246
01:56:57,226 --> 01:57:06,439
The first is empty file detection, and it matters more than you'd initially guess,

1247
01:57:06,728 --> 01:57:13,308
because a command that silently fails still creates a file.

1248
01:57:13,598 --> 01:57:22,810
It's zero bytes, but it still shows up in a directory listing looking present,

1249
01:57:23,100 --> 01:57:26,390
looking fine, proving absolutely nothing.

1250
01:57:26,679 --> 01:57:35,892
This exact failure mode is precisely what reaches real auditors, because it slides right

1251
01:57:36,181 --> 01:57:43,419
past any naive check that only asks does the file exist.

1252
01:57:43,709 --> 01:57:52,921
Our script checks file size specifically, not just file presence, for every single control

1253
01:57:53,211 --> 01:57:54,111
directory.

1254
01:57:54,400 --> 01:58:02,296
The second is that the script's exit code is meaningful on purpose.

1255
01:58:02,586 --> 01:58:07,192
Zero means the package is genuinely complete.

1256
01:58:07,481 --> 01:58:11,430
One means there are real gaps.

1257
01:58:11,719 --> 01:58:20,931
That means this composes cleanly into a CI pipeline, you can gate a release

1258
01:58:21,221 --> 01:58:30,433
on it, or simply run it every night and alert only on failure, without

1259
01:58:30,723 --> 01:58:39,277
a single human ever needing to manually open the directory and eyeball it.

1260
01:58:39,566 --> 01:58:48,779
Now let's have an honest conversation about build versus buy, because pretending this trade

1261
01:58:49,068 --> 01:58:58,281
off doesn't exist helps nobody, and glossing over it is exactly the kind of

1262
01:58:58,570 --> 01:59:07,783
thing that makes a course feel written by someone who's never actually had this

1263
01:59:08,072 --> 01:59:11,362
conversation with a real client.

1264
01:59:11,652 --> 01:59:20,864
Platforms like Vanta, Drata, and Secureframe exist, and they are genuinely useful for plenty

1265
01:59:21,153 --> 01:59:30,366
of teams, especially smaller ones running fairly standard SaaS infrastructure who want a GRC

1266
01:59:30,655 --> 01:59:39,868
platform with built in auditor workflows and meaningfully less custom tooling to maintain themselves.

1267
01:59:40,157 --> 01:59:49,370
Buy, when your team is small, your infrastructure is mostly standard, and speed to

1268
01:59:49,659 --> 01:59:57,556
your first Type One matters more than owning the entire pipeline yourself.

1269
01:59:57,845 --> 02:00:07,058
Build, which is exactly what we did across this whole phase, when your infrastructure

1270
02:00:07,347 --> 02:00:16,559
doesn't map cleanly onto a GRC platform's connector model, because in cluster evidence like

1271
02:00:16,849 --> 02:00:26,061
Falco alerts or ArgoCD sync status rarely has a proper first class connector on

1272
02:00:26,351 --> 02:00:35,563
any of these platforms, and because you genuinely want your own engineers to understand

1273
02:00:35,852 --> 02:00:45,065
these controls deeply, rather than treating compliance as a dashboard some outside vendor quietly

1274
02:00:45,354 --> 02:00:47,987
maintains on your behalf.

1275
02:00:48,276 --> 02:00:51,566
Both paths are completely legitimate.

1276
02:00:51,855 --> 02:01:01,068
Recommending one without first asking about team size and infrastructure shape is the mark

1277
02:01:01,357 --> 02:01:10,570
of someone who hasn't actually run this trade off with a real paying client.

1278
02:01:10,859 --> 02:01:20,072
Let's zoom all the way out and see how this entire phase actually fits

